Deputies of the Assembly of Wales are afraid to “go through Muppets”
There is dismay by some deputies of the Assembly of Wales (AM) regarding the suggestion that their title be changed to MWP (Deputies of the Welsh Parliament).
The origin of this controversy lies in the plans to change the name of the assembly to "Welsh Parliament".
Some members of that House, coming from all political colours, are afraid that this will make them fall into ridicule.
A Labour AM stated that the group is concerned that “the designation rhymes with Twp and Pwp.”
For non-Welsh readers: in the Welsh language, “twp” means “idiot” and pwp means “excrement.”
An AM of Plaid stated that the group as a whole “was not happy” and suggested alternatives.
A Welsh Conservative MP stated that the group was “open-minded” in relation to changing the name, but said that the designation MWP was verbally very close to Muppet (Marreta).
In this context, the letter w in Welsh has a pronunciation similar to that of the letter u in Yorkshire English.
The Assembly's Committee, currently drafting legislation to introduce the name changes, declared: "The final decision on how Assembly Deputies are designated will obviously be taken by themselves."
The Wales Government Act of 2017 has given its assembly the powers to change its name.
In June, the Commission published the results of a public consultation on the proposals, which received widespread support for the proposal to designate the assembly as the "Welsh Parliament".
On the question of the title of the AM, the Commission favoured "Members of the Welsh Parliament", or WMP, but the MWP option was more favoured in a public consultation.
Apparently, AMs are suggesting alternatives, but the effort to gather consensus may constitute a headache for the President, Elin Jones, who is expected to present a draft legislation on the amendments within a few weeks.
Reform legislation will include other changes to how the House carries out its activities, such as rules on the disqualification of AMs and the configuration of the committee system.
When they discuss the legislation, AMs will have the final say on the question of how they should be designated.
Macedonians to endorse the country's name change
Voters will vote on Sunday on changing their country’s name to “Republic of North Macedonia.”
The popular vote was organized in an attempt to resolve a decades-long conflict with neighbouring Greece, which is part of a province named after Macedonia.
Athens has long insisted that its northern neighbour's name constitutes a complaint about its territory, having repeatedly objected to its applications for EU and NATO membership.
The Macedonian president Gjorge Ivanov, opponent of the plebiscite on the name change, declared that he will ignore the vote.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that changing the name is nothing more than the price to pay for EU and NATO membership.
St. Martin's bells shut up at a time when Harlem churches fight for survival
“In a historical perspective, the seniors with whom I spoke told me that there was a bar and a church on every corner,” Mr. Adams said.
“Today, there’s neither.”
The disappearance of the bars, he said, was understood.
“People socialize differently,” he said today.
"Bars have ceased to be the living rooms of the neighborhood where people regularly head."
As far as the churches are concerned, it worries the possibility that the money earned from the sale of heritage does not last as long as the leaders expect” and that, sooner or later, everything will return exactly to the starting point.”
The churches, he added, can be replaced by apartment buildings with condominiums inhabited by the type of people who will not give support to the remaining neighborhood sanctuaries.
“The overwhelming majority of the people who buy apartments in these buildings will be white,” he said, “and, therefore, this will hasten the arrival of the day when these churches will all close, for it is unlikely that most of those who move into these complexes will join these congregations.”
Both churches were built by white congregations before Harlem became a black metropolis: the Metropolitan Community in 1870 and St. Martin's a decade later.
The original white Methodist congregation came out of here in the 1930s.
The building was later sworn in by a black congregation that held its services in the immediate vicinity.
St. Martin's was occupied by a black congregation under the auspices of Reverend John Howard Johnson, who led a boycott of 125th Street shopkeepers, an important shopping street in Harlem that resisted the hiring and promotion of black employees.
A fire in 1939 left the building badly damaged. However, when they made the plans for its reconstruction, Father Johnson's parishioners commissioned the carillon.
Rev. David Johnson, the son of Father Johnson and his successor at St. Martin’s, proudly called the carillon “the bells of the poor.”
The expert who played the carillon in July called it something else: “a cultural treasure” and an “irreplaceable historical instrument.”
This expert, Tiffany Ng of the University of Michigan, also mentioned that it was the world’s first carillon to be played by a black musician, Dionisio A. Lind, who moved into the larger carillon at Riverside Church 18 years ago.
Merriweather claimed St. Martin's didn't replace him.
What has occurred in St. Martin's in recent months has been a complicated history of architects and contractors, some brought by the secular leaders of the church and others by the episcopal diocese.
The parish council, the governing body of the parish composed of secular leaders, wrote to the diocese in July, expressing its concern that it “would try to pass on its costs”, badly the fact that it was not involved in the hiring of the architects and contractors sent by the diocese.
Some parishioners complained of a lack of transparency on the part of the diocese.
Shark Causes Injuries to 13-Year-Old Teenager in California Lobster-hunting Dive
A shark attacked and wounded a 13-year-old boy on Saturday during a lobster-hunting dive in California on the first day of the lobster season, authorities said.
The attack occurred shortly after 7 a.m. near Beacon's Beach in Encinitas.
Chad Hammel told KSWB-TV in San Diego that he had been diving with friends for about half an hour when he heard the boy scream for help, having then rowed towards him with a group to help him out of the water.
Hammel stated that he initially thought that it was only enthusiasm because he caught a lobster, but that he later realized that the boy shouted "I was bitten"!
I've been bitten!
The whole area of the collarbone was torn apart, Hammel described what he observed when he arrived at the boy.
"Screamed everyone to get out of the water: "There's a shark in the water!" added Hammel.
The boy was transported by air to Rady Children's Hospital in San Diego, where he was admitted in critical condition.
It is unknown the species of the shark responsible for the attack.
Lifelavor Captain Larry Giles stated in a press release that a shark had been spotted in the area a few weeks earlier, but that it was considered not to belong to a dangerous species.
Giles added that the victim suffered traumatic injuries to the upper trunk.
Officials closed access from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami's in Ecinitas for 48 hours, for investigative purposes and for security reasons.
Giles said there are more than 135 species of sharks in the area, but most are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury's plans to enter the UK cosmetics market
Sainsbury's is competing with Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams with large warehouse-style cosmetic sections, backed by expert assistants.
As part of a substantial initiative to gain share in the UK cosmetics market, valued at 2.8 billion pounds, which has continued to grow while sales of fashion apparel and house goods retreat, these larger cosmetics sections will be tested in 11 stores around the country and will be implemented in more stores next year if their success is demonstrated.
This investment in cosmetics stems from the search, by supermarkets, for ways to use exhibition space once used for TVs, microwaves and household items.
Sainsbury's mentioned that it would duplicate its catalogue of beauty products with the offer of up to 3000 products, including brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw for the first time.
L'Oréal, Maybelline and Burt's Bees ranges will also get more space with brand areas similar to Boots stores.
The supermarket chain is also relaunching its range of Boutique makeup so that most products obey vegan precepts, something increasingly demanded by younger buyers.
In addition, The Fragrance Shop will test concessions at two Sainsbury's stores, the first of which opened in Croydon, south of London, last week. It is predicted that the latter, at Selly Oak in Birmingham, will open near the end of the year.
Online shopping and changing food shopping habits for small daily amounts in nearby stores have led supermarkets to now have to try harder to persuade customers to visit their stores.
Mike Coupe, executive chairman of Sainsbury's, said the stores will increasingly resemble large warehouses, at a time when the supermarket chain is trying to combat the Aldi and Lidl discount stores with more services and non-food products.
Sainsbury's has been installing Argos points of sale in hundreds of stores, having also installed a number of Habitats, since it acquired both chains two years ago, and claims that this reinforced the sales of grocery products and made the acquisitions more profitable.
The company’s previous attempt to renovate its cosmetics and pharmacy departments ended in failure.
Sainsbury's tested a partnership with Boots in the early 2000s, but the relationship ended after a dispute over the distribution of revenues from healthcare stores in their supermarkets.
The new strategy comes after Sainsbury's sold three years ago its 281-point pharmacy business to Celesio, which owns the Lloyds Pharmacy chain, for 125 million pounds.
It was said by the company that Lloyds would participate in the plan by adding in four stores a wide range of luxury skincare brands such as La Roche-Posay and Vichy.
Paul Mills-Hicks, Chief Commercial Officer of Sainsbury's, said: "We've transformed the look and essence of our cosmetics sections to provide our customers with a more pleasant environment.
We also invest in the specialized training of colleagues, who will be available to advise the client.
Our range of brands is designed to adapt to every need. On the other hand, the inviting environment and practical locations make us now an irresistible point of sale of beauty products that breaks with the old ways of shopping."
Peter Jones 'furious' after Holly Willoughby abandoned 11-million-pound deal
Peter Jones, star of Dragons Den, became "furious" after TV host Holly Willoughby abandoned an 11-million-pound deal with his lifestyle brand to focus on his new contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV
Willoughby doesn't have time for homeware brand and Truly accessories.
Their business had been compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand.
The 37-year-old This Morning host announced on Instagram that she was going to abandon the partnership.
Holly Willoughby left Dragons Star Den Peter Jones livid by abandoning the lucratious brand at the last minute to focus on his highly profitable new contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
Some sources claim that Jones was "furious" when TV's golden girl admitted, during a tense reunion last Tuesday at the headquarters of her business empire in Marlow, Buckinghamshire, that her new businesses (whose value may amount to 1.5 million pounds), implied that she would no longer have time to dedicate herself to her brand of household items and Truly accessories.
The deal was compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand and was expected to double Willoughby's fortune, estimated at 11 million pounds.
While Willoughby, 37, announced on Instagram that he would leave Truly, Jones would take a plane to one of his holiday homes abroad.
One source claimed: "Truly was by far Holly's biggest priorities.
It would mean the resolution of its long-term future and it would be financial support for the next two decades.
His decision to leave made everyone absolutely amazed.
No one believed in what was happening on Tuesday... it was so close to launch.
There’s a warehouse full of ready-to-sell items at Marlow headquarters.”
Experts believe the departure of the host of This Morning, which is among Britain's most lucratious vedetas, could cost the company millions, given the large investment in products, ranging from cushions and candles to apparel and household items, and the potential for further launch delays.
In addition, it could mean the end of a long friendship.
Willoughby, with three children, and husband Dan Baldwin, have been close to Jones and his wife Tara Capp for ten years.
Willoughby created Truly with Capp in 2016 and Jones, aged 52, joined the project as president in March.
Couples vacation together and Jones owns a 40 percent share at Baldwin’s television production company.
Willoughby is about to become brand ambassador for M&S and will replace Ant McPartlin as host of ITV's I'm A Celebrity.
A source close to Jones said last night that "We don't intend to comment on his business."
Tough conversation "and then we fell in love"
The U.S. president jokingly mentioned the criticism that he would be targeted by the media for making a comment that some would consider "unworthy of a president" and for revealing such a positive attitude with the North Korean leader.
Why did President Trump relent on so much?
Trump claimed, in his voice pretended to "pivot of telejournal."
“I didn’t give in to anything.”
He said that Kim is interested in a second meeting after his initial meeting in Singapore in June and stressed that it would be a major step in the denuclearization of North Korea.
However, negotiations for denuclearization are dragging on.
More than three months after the June summit in Singapore, most important North Korean diplomat Ri Yong Ho told world leaders at the U.N. General Assembly that the North does not see a "responding response" from the U.S
Instead, he said, the U.S
Trump adopted a more optimistic point of view in his speech at the rally.
“It’s going very well with North Korea,” he said.
“We were about to go to war with North Korea.
Millions of people would have been killed.
We now have this excellent relationship.”
Trump said his efforts to improve relations with Kim brought positive results: an end to tests with rockets, support for the release of hostages and the return to the country of the remains of the U.S. military.
In addition, he defended his unusual approach when he spoke in relations with Kim.
"It's so easy to be worthy of a president, but instead of having 10 000 people outside trying to get into this overcrowded arena, we'd have about 200 people standing right here," Trump said, pointing to the crowd directly ahead of him.
Tsunami and earthquake in Indonesia cause devastation on an island, causing the deaths of hundreds
