Although more than 10% of Lombok's population had been dislodged, no national catastrophe has been declared, a prerequisite for channelling international aid.
"Unfortunately, in many cases it was clear that they are not requesting international aid, so it becomes a little difficult," Ms Sumbung claimed.
Although Save the Children is organising a team heading to Palu, there is still no guarantee that foreign personnel will be able to work on the ground.
Mr Sutopo, a spokesman for the national disaster response agency, stated that Indonesian authorities are assessing the situation in Palu to determine whether international agencies would be allowed to contribute to the aid effort.
Despite Indonesia's constant earthquakes suffered, the country remains terribly ill-prepared for nature's anger.
Although they were built in Aceh, tsunami shelters aren't commonly sighted on other coastlines.
It's likely that the apparent lack of a mermaid tsunami alarm in Palu, badly the validity of a warning, had contributed to the loss of life.
In the most favorable times, traveling among the many Indonesian islands is a challenge.
Natural disasters make logistics even more complicated.
A hospital ship stationed in Lombok to treat earthquake victims is on its way to Palu, but it will take at least three days to arrive at the site of this new calamity.
President Joko Widodo, who has made improving the decrepit infrastructure a centerpiece of his election campaign, has spent huge amounts on roads and railways.
However, the scarcity of funding plagued Mr Joko's executive, at a time facing his re-election next year.
The president is also being pressured by persistent sectarian tensions in Indonesia, where members of the Muslim majority have embraced a more conservative form of their faith.
More than 1000 people died and tens of thousands were dislodged as Christian and Muslim gangs fought in the streets using canas, bows and arrows and other rudimentary weapons.
See: Liverpool's Daniel Sturridge makes a long shot for draw goal against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge saved a Liverpool from a defeat in the Premier League against Chelsea with an 89th-minute goal on Saturday at London's Stamford Bridge stadium.
Sturridge received a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri when he was about 30 metres from Chelsea's beacon, with his team losing 1-0.
He touched his left before making a shot towards the second beam.
In the attempt, the ball flew high over the large area when heading to the upper right corner of the net.
In the end, the ball crashed over a Kepa Arrizabalaga in flight and crashed inside the net.
"I was just trying to hit that position, to get to the ball, and the players like Shaq advance as much as possible, so I was just winning as long as possible," Sturridge told LiverpoolFC.com.
"I saw Kante coming in, I gave it a touch and I didn't think too much about it. I just shot it."
Chelsea won 1-0 at halftime after scoring a goal in the 25th minute from Belgian star Eden Hazard.
On that move, the blue forward took a heeling pass back to Mateo Kovacic before unraveling near midfield and effecting a sprint towards the Liverpool area.
Kovacic made a quick midfield pass.
He then effected a beautiful shot that crossed the opposing defense, leading Hazard to the large area.
The latter surpassed the defenders and made the goal against the net of the second crossbar with a shot with his left foot that went through Liverpool's Alisson Becker goal.
Liverpool face Naples in the Champions League group stage at three on Wednesday afternoon at the San Paolo Stadium in Naples, Italy.
Chelesse will face Videoton in the UEFA Europa League at three on Thursday afternoon in London.
Indonesia tsunami death toll rises to 832
Indonesia’s death toll in Indonesia’s quake and tsunami rose to 832, the country’s disaster response agency said on Sunday.
There are reports describing a large number of people trapped in the wreckage of buildings that collapsed during the 7.5-magnitude quake on Friday, which unleashed waves that peaked at 6 meters high, Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, the agency’s spokeswoman, said at a press conference.
The city of Palu, with more than 380 000 inhabitants, was covered with the rubble of the collapsed buildings.
Police arrest 32-year-old man on suspicion of murder after stabbing a woman to death
A homicide investigation was launched after a woman's body was found this morning in Birkenhead on Merseyside.
The 44-year-old victim was found at 7:55 a.m
Police urged the population of the area who may have witnessed something giving information.
Detective Inspector Brian O'Hagan said: "The investigation is at an early stage, but I'd like to appeal to everyone in the immediate vicinity of John Street in Birkenheaden and who witnessed something suspicious to contact us.
I also want to appeal to those who may have obtained images with tablier cameras, particularly taxi drivers, to contact us, because they may have crucial information for the investigation."
A police spokesman confirmed that the woman whose body was found was resident in Birkenhead and was located inside a property.
This afternoon, friends who believe they know the victim headed to the scene of the incident to inquire about where she had been found this morning.
Investigations are ongoing, with police saying they are currently informing the victim's close relatives.
A taxi driver living in Grayson Mews has just tried to access his apartment, but has been told by police that entrances or exits from the building are prohibited.
When he was informed about what had happened, he was speechless.
Residents are now being informed that they will only be able to return to their homes within hours.
A police officer has been heard telling a man that the entirety of the area is now being treated as a crime scene.
A woman arrived at the scene bathed in tears.
It repeatedly states that "it's so horrifying."
At two in the afternoon, two police vans were inside the cordon and another was parked in the area immediately outside.
A number of police officers inside the cordon have been deployed to guard the apartment building.
