Cromwell's statue of Parliament has become the latest monument engulfed by contention of "historical revisionism"
Its removal would constitute an act of poetic justice for its destruction, reminiscent of that perpetrated by the Taliban, of so many English cultural and religious artifacts, and which was carried out by their fanatical Puritan followers.
However the Cromwell Society described Crick's suggestion as "a madness" and "an attempt to rewrite history."
John Goldsmith, president of the Cromwell Society, stated: "It was inevitable that, in the current debate on the removal of statues, that Oliver Cromwell's figure next to the Palace of Westminster would become a target to cull.
The iconoclasm of the English civil wars was neither ordered nor carried out by Cromwell.
Perhaps the wrong Cromwell is being sacrificed for the acts of his ancestor Thomas in the previous century.
Cromwell’s magnificent depiction, authored by Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft, is a testament to 19th-century opinion and part of historiography regarding a figure that many still believe to be worthy of homage.
Goldsmith told the Sunday Telegraph: "Cromwell is regarded by many, perhaps more at the end of the 19th century than today, as a supporter of parliament against external pressures, in his case, of course, the monarchy.
Whether or not this constitutes an entirely precise description is the subject of a continuing historical debate.
What's certain is that the mid-17th-century conflict shaped the subsequent development of our nation, and that Cromwell is a figure with individual notoriety that represents a side of that division.
His successes as Lord Protector are also worthy of homage and commemoration."
Murdered pork attacks and kills Chinese farmer
A farmer has been attacked and killed by a pig in a southwestern Chinese market, according to local media reports.
The man, identified only by his nickname "Yuan," was found dead, with an artery severed and covered in blood, near a pocilga at the market in Liupanshui, Guizhou province, reads Sunday's South China Morning Post.
The pig farmer prepares to inject vaccines into piggies in a pig farm on May 30, 2005 in Xining in Qinghai Province, China.
According to reports, he traveled with his cousin from the neighboring province of Yunnan to sell 15 pigs on the market.
The next morning, his cousin found him dead and discovered a door to an open neighboring pickle.
In that pocilga, he said, there was a large male pig with blood in his mouth.
A forensic analysis confirmed that the pig, with 227 kilograms, had attacked the pig farmer to death, according to the report.
“My cousin’s legs were covered in blood and crushed, said the cousin, referred to by his surname ‘Wu,’ according to Guiyang Evening News.
Security camera footage shows Yuan entering the market at 4:40 a.m. on Thursday to feed his pigs.
His body was discovered about an hour later.
The animal that killed the man didn't belong to Yuan or his cousin.
A market manager told Evening News that the pig had been closed to prevent it from attacking other people while police collected evidence at the scene.
Reports say Yuan’s family and market authorities are negotiating compensation for his death.
Although rare, cases of attacks by pigs on humans have already been reported.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband on his farm in Massachusetts, leaving the man with serious injuries.
Ten years earlier, a 300-pound pig immobilized a Welsh farming against his tractor until the woman managed to scare away the animal.
After an Oregon farm had been eaten by his pigs in 2012, a Manitoba farmer told CBC News that pigs are not normally aggressive, but that the smell of the blood can function as "stimulus."
"They're just joking.
They like pinching, they're very curious... they don't want to hurt anyone.
We just have to show them due respect," he said.
Remnants of Hurricane Rosa Are Expected To Bring Spiraged Heavy Rain to Southwestern United States
As predicted, Hurricane Rosa is weakening as it travels over the coldest waters off Mexico’s northern coast.
However, it will bring torrential rains to the entire Mexican north and southwestern U.S. over the next few days.
At 5 a.m., East Hour on Sunday, Rosa caused gusts of 137 km/h, a Category 1, hurricane located 620 km southwest of Punta Eugenia in Mexico.
Rosa is expected to move north on Sunday.
However, it begins to form a depression over the Pacific Ocean moving to the west coast of the U.S. As it approaches the Baja California peninsula, the Rosa, now a tropical storm, will begin to push a strong tropical humidity toward the north in the southwestern U.S.
On Monday, it will cause precipitation that can reach 25 mm in certain areas of Mexico.
At that time, the tropical humidity interacting with the approaching depression will produce widespread heavy rainfall in the Southwest in the coming days.
Locally, 2.5 to 10 mm of rain could cause dangerous sudden flooding, flooding with debris, and possibly landfills in the desert.
The strong tropical humidity will cause precipitation that can reach 5 to 7 mm in one-off zones, especially in parts of southern Nevada and Arizona.
Up to 5 to 10 mm of precipitation is expected in Southwestern areas, especially over much of Arizona.
Sudden flooding is possible with rapidly worsening conditions due to the dispersed nature of tropical rainfall.
It's highly inadvisable to walk in the desert on foot with the threat of tropical rainfall.
Strong precipitation can cause the canyons to convert into brave rivers and the thunderstorms will bring strong one-off winds and clouds of dust.
The approaching depression will bring with it strong precipitation over parts of the Southern California coastline.
Total rainfall of more than 12 mm is possible, which can cause small floods of debris and slippery roads.
This may be the first precipitation of the region’s wet season.
Some dispersed tropical downpours will begin in Arizona in late Sunday and early Monday, before rain becomes more dispersed in late Monday and Tuesday.
Heavy rain will spread at the Four Corners on Tuesday and last until Wednesday.
The month of October can register strong temperature variations throughout the U.S. territory as the Arctic cools. However, the tropical zones will remain quite mild.
Sometimes this leads to drastic temperature changes over short distances.
There is an excellent example of drastic temperature differences throughout the U.S. central zone on Sunday.
There's an almost 11-degree difference between Kansas City in Missouri and Omaha in Nebraska and between St. Louis and Des Moines in Iowa.
In the coming days, persistent summer heat will tend to escalate and widen again.
Much of the U.S. central and eastern zone is expected to record a warm start in October, with widespread temperatures between 27 and 30 degrees from the Southern Plains to parts of the Northeast.
New York City could reach 27 degrees on Tuesday, which would be about 5.5 degrees above average.
Our long-term weather forecast indicates a high probability of above-average temperatures in the U.S
More than 20 million people have attended Brett Kavanaugh's hearing
More than 20 million people watched yesterday on six television stations the sweeping testimony of Brett Kavanaugh, appointed to the Supreme Court, and of the woman who accused him of sexual abuse allegedly occurred in the 1980s, Christine Blasey Ford.
However, the political impasse continued, with stations halting regular programming due to a last-minute twist: an agreement drafted by Arizona Senator Jeff Flake for the FBI to conduct a week-long investigation into the charges.
Ford told the Senate Judiciary Committee that he is absolutely sure that Kavanaugh groped her while drunk and tried to undress her at a high school party.
Kavanaugh, in an inflamed testimony, said he is absolutely sure that this did not happen.
More than 20.4 million people are likely to be estimated by Nielsen on Friday to have attended the hearing.
The company accounted for the average number of viewers on CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC chains.
The numbers of the other stations that transmitted it, such as PBS, C-SPAN and the Fox Business Network, were not yet available.
In addition, Nielsen usually struggles to measure audiences of those who see programs in offices.
To give some perspective, it is a level of audiences similar to the final of an American football game or that of the Oscar ceremony.
The Fox News Channel, whose commentators gave their strong support for Kavanaugh's appointment, led all television stations with an average of 5.69 million viewers during the all-day hearing, Nielsen said.
ABC ranked second with 3.26 million viewers.
Next comes CBS with 3.1 million, NBC with 2.94 million, MSNBC with 2.89 million and CNN with 2.52 million, Nielsen said.
Interest remained high after the hearing.
Flake was the central figure of Friday's drama.
After the moderate Republican's office issued a statement stating that his vote would be in favor of Kavanaugh, the senator was picked up by CNN and CBS chambers on Friday morning being verbally attacked by protesters as he tried to enter an elevator to address a hearing of the Judicial Commission.
The senator remained motionless looking at the ground as he was criticized straight to CNN's cameras.
"I'm standing here in front of you," one woman said.
"Do you think he's telling the country the truth?
He was told that "you have power while so many women don't have any power."
Flake stated that his office had issued a statement and said, before the elevator door closed, that he would have more to say at the commission's hearing.
Cable and broadcasting stations were all covering the event live hours later, when the Judicial Commission went to votes to make Kavanaugh's nomination proceed to a vote in Senate plenary session.
However, Flake stated that he would only do so in the understanding that the FBI would investigate the accusations against the nominee over the next week, which the Democratic minority had been demanding.
Flake was partly convinced after conversations with his friend, Democratic Senator Chris Coons.
After a conversation with Coons and subsequently with a few other senators, he made his decision.
Flake's choice was decisive, since it was evident that Republicans wouldn't have the votes to pass Kavanaugh without the investigation.
President Trump has opened an investigation by the FBI into the allegations against Kavanaugh.
Prime Minister May accuses critics of 'making politiquice' with Brexit
Prime Minister Theresa May accused critics of her plans to leave the European Union to "make politice" about Britain's future and harm the national interest, in an interview with the Sunny Times.
Theresa May, the British prime minister, arrives at the Conservative Party Conference in Birmingham, Britain, on September 29, 2018.
In another interview with the newspaper's front-page interview, its former foreign minister Boris Johnson reinforced his attack on what is known as "Chequers Plan" of the Brexit minister, stating that a proposal predicting that Britain and the EU will cover the other party's tariffs is "totally absurd."
Wayde Sims murder: police detain suspect Dyteon Simpson from LSU player's death
Police have arrested a suspect in the deadly shooting of Sims, a 20-year-old basketball player at LSU.
Dyteon Simpson, aged 20, was arrested and charged with second-degree murder, according to a report by the Baton Rouge Police Department.
Officials have released a video of the confrontation between Sims and Simpson, with police claiming that Sims lost his glasses during the fight.
Police retrieved them at the scene of the incident and declared that they had found on them Simpson's DNA, WAFB, a subsidiary of CBS reports.
After interrogating Simpson, the police reported that he had fatally hit Wayde with a firearm.
A bail of 350 000 USD has been established, the lawyer said.
East Baton Rouge's parish medical-legal office issued a preliminary report stating that the cause of death was a gunshot wound to the head that stretched to the neck.
The department reports that the collaboration of the Louisiana State Police action group, the state police crime lab, the Southern University police and area residents who collaborated in the investigation led to this detention.
LSU's athletic director, Joe Alleva, thanked area authorities for their "diligence and effort to get justice."
Sims was 20 years old.
This forward, 1.98 m tall, grew up in Baton Rouge, where his father, Wayne, also played basketball for LSU.
His average was 5.6 points and 2.6 rebounds per game last season.
On Friday morning, LSU basketball coach Will Wade claimed the team was "devastated" and "in shock" over Wayde's death.
“This is what worries us permanently,” Wade said.
Volcano ejects ashes in Mexico City
Emanant ashes from Popocatepetl volcano hit the neighborhoods south of Mexico's capital.
The National Center for the Prevention of Catastrophes on Sunday advised Mexicans to stay away from the volcano after a resumption of activity in the crater, which recorded 183 gas and ash emissions within 24-hours.
The center spotted numerous deaf noises and tremors.
Footage on social media showed thin layers of grey covering the windscreens of automobiles in neighborhoods of Mexico City like Xochimilco.
Geophysicists recorded an increase in volcano activity, located 72 kilometers from the capital, since a 7.1-magnitude earthquake shook central Mexico in September 2017.
The volcano, known as "Don Goyo", has recorded activity since 1994.
Clashes between police and Catalan separatists ahead of the anniversary of vote for independence
Six people were detained in Barcelona on Saturday after clashes between independence protesters and intervention police, as thousands of people joined rival demonstrations to mark the first anniversary of the polarous Catalan referendum on secession.
A group of masqueraded separatists, caught by intervention police, struck her with eggs and threw powdered paint, creating dark clouds of dust on streets that are normally teeming with tourists.
Clashes also occurred later, in which the police resorted to batons to contain the conflicts.
For a few hours, independentist groups chanting "We didn't forget, we didn't forgive" confronted unionist demonstrators, who chanted "Long life for Spain."
Fourteen people have received treatment of minor injuries sustained in the protests, it can be read in the local press.
Tensions remain elevated in the region inclined towards independence, a year after the October 1 referendum that was considered illegal by Madrid but which was celebrated by Catalan separatists.
Voters overwhelmingly chose independence, even though turnout to the polls had been low, with a majority boycott of those who are against.
According to the Catalan authorities, almost 1000 people were injured last year after police tried to block the ongoing vote at polling stations scattered around the region, which resulted in violent clashes.
The independence groups had camped overnight on Friday to prevent a rally of support for national police.
Although it was held, this demonstration was forced to take a different path.
Narcis Termes, 68, an electrician taking part in the separatist protest with his wife, said he no longer had any hope that Catalonia would become independent.
“Last year, we experienced our best moments.
“I’ve seen my parents cry with joy that they can vote, but now we’re blocked,” he said.
Despite having won a vital but tangential victory in regional elections last December, Catalan independence parties have struggled to maintain momentum this year, with their best-known leaders in voluntary exile or in detention awaiting trial for their role in organising the referendum and subsequent declaration of independence.
Joan Puig, a 42-year-old mechanic who recorded the protest in support of the police with his mobile phone, said the conflict was fueled by politicians on both sides.
"It's becoming increasingly tense," he said.
On Saturday, Oriol Junqueras, one of the nine Catalan leaders detained awaiting trial since last year, announced that he would run for the European Parliament in next year's elections.
"Presenting myself as a candidate for the European elections is the best way to denounce the regression of democratic values and the repression we have seen on the part of the Spanish government," he said.
