In addition, in October last year, intervention police officers were summoned to resolve a serious disturbance in which personnel were attacked with billiard balls.
Hurricane Rosa threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City with flash flooding (drug-affected areas may benefit)
It's rarely a tropical depression hit Arizona, but that's exactly what's predicted for early next week, when the remaining energy of Hurricane Rosa traverses the Desert Southwest, implying the risk of flash flooding.
The National Meteorological Service has already issued flash flood warnings for Monday and Tuesday, to the southern and east of Nevada, to Southern California and Utah, including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas, and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to take a direct trajectory over Phoenix on Tuesday, approaching late Monday with rainfall.
The National Meteorological Service in Phoenix said in a tweet that only "ten tropical cyclones have maintained a tropical state or depression within 300 km of Phoenix since 1950!
Katrina (1967) was a hurricane that passed 60 km from the Arizona border.
The latest models from the National Hurricane Center predict 5 to 10 mm of precipitation, with isolated quantities that can reach 15 mm in Arizona's Mogollon Rim.
Other areas of the Soutwest Desert, such as the Central Rocky Mountains and the Large Basin may receive 3 to 5 mm, with the possibility of isolated totals that can reach 10 mm.
For those unaffected by the risks of sudden flooding, rose rain can constitute a blessing, as the region is affected by drought.
Although the floods constitute a very serious concern, some of this precipitation may be beneficial, as the Southwest is currently being affected by the drought.
According to U.S. Drought Monitor, just over 40 percent of Arizona suffers at least from extreme drought, the second-most serious category," weather.com reported.
Firstly, Hurricane Rosa will hit the continental mass by crossing the Baja California peninsula in Mexico.
The Rosa, still with hurricane force on Sunday morning, with winds at maximum speeds of 137 km/h, lies 619 km south of Punta Eugenia in Mexico, moving north at a speed of 19 kilometers per hour.
The storm is finding cooler waters in the Pacific, so it's weakening.
Thus, it is expected to hit the coastal zone in Mexico with tropical storm force in the afternoon or by Monday night.
There may be heavy precipitation in parts of Mexico, which poses a significant risk of flooding.
“Total rainfall of 8 to 10 mm from Baja California to the northwest of Sonora is expected, with a maximum of 25 mm possible,” weather.com said in a statement.
The Rose will then head north, crossing Mexico as a tropical storm before hitting the Arizona border in the early hours of Tuesday morning as a tropical depression. It will then head to southern Utah in the last hours of Tuesday night.
“The main expected danger of the Rose or its remnants is very high rainfall in Baja California, northwestern Sonora, and in the U.S. Desert Southwest,” the National Hurricane Center said.
It is anticipated that these rains will produce sudden flooding and floods of debris in life-threatening deserts and flooding on mountainous terrain.
