Hundreded in earthquake and tsunami in Indonesia; number continues to rise
At least 384 people have died, many of them swept away by the gigantic waves that hit beaches, when a major earthquake and tsunami hit the Indonesian island of Sulawesi, authorities said on Saturday.
Hundreds of people had gathered for a beach festival in the city of Palu on Friday, when waves up to six feet high crashed on the beach at dusk, dragging many to death and causing a trail of devastation on their way.
The Tsunamis occurred in the wake of a 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
"When the threat of a tsunami arose yesterday, people were still in their activities on the beach and did not flee immediately, having been the victims of what happened," said Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, the agency's spokesman to mitigate the effect of Indonesia's catastrophes (BNPB) in a communication in Jakarta.
"The tsunami did not move alone: it dragged cars, trunks, houses, hit everything on land," Nugroho said, adding that the tsunami was moving in the open sea at speeds of 800 km/h before reaching the coastline.
Some people climbed trees to escape the tsunami and survived, he said.
Nearly 16 700 people have been evacuated to 24 centers in Palu.
Aerial photographs published by the agency show many destroyed buildings and shops, deformed and slaughtered bridges, and a mosque surrounded by water.
The coastal town continued to be shaken by aftershocks on Saturday.
The series of earthquakes was felt in an area with 2.4 million inhabitants.
The Agency for the Evaluation and Application of Technology (BPPT) stated in a statement that the energy released by Friday's huge shake-up was about 200 times the power of the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima in World War II.
The geography of the city, located at the end of a long and narrow bay, may have amplified the size of the tsunami.
Nugroho described the damage as "extensive" and said thousands of homes, hospitals, shopping centres and hotels collapsed.
Bodies have been found of some victims trapped under the rubble of the collapsed buildings, it said, adding that 540 people were injured and 29 are missing.
Nugroho said the number of casualties and the damage may be deeper along the coastline 300 km north of Palu, an area named Donggala, closer to the epicenter of the quake.
Communications "were totally incapacitated and there was no information" about Donggala, Nugroho said.
There are more than 300 000 inhabitants in the area," the Red Cross said in a statement, adding that its staff and volunteers were heading for the affected areas.
“This is already a tragedy, but it could get much worse,” he said.
On Saturday, the agency was widely criticised for failing to report that a tsunami had hit Palu, although officials claimed the waves emerged at the time the warning was issued.
In an amateur footage released on social media, you can see a man on the upper floor of a building shouting frantic warnings that the tsunami was approaching, for the people down the street.
In a space of minutes, a wall of water fell on the coast, dragging buildings and automobiles.
Reuters was unable to immediately confirm the authenticity of the footage.
The quake and tsunami caused an extensive power cut that disrupted communications in the Palu area, which made it difficult for authorities to coordinate salvage efforts.
The army began sending cargo aircraft with help from Jakarta and other cities. However, evacuees still desperately need food and other basic goods.
The city's airport has been reopened only for salvage efforts and will remain closed until October.
President Joko Widodo scheduled a visit to Palau's evacuation centres on Sunday.
The death toll in Indonesia's tsunami rises exponentially to more than 800.
It's really bad.
Although it has managed to safely reach the city of Palu, where employees are housed in canvas shelters set up in the courtyard of its offices, Donggala’s World Vision staff has gone through scenes of devastation on its way, Mr. Doseba said.
"I was told they've seen a lot of houses destroyed," he said.
It's really bad.
Even as the aid groups began to start the grim tasks of assisting the victims of the catastrophe, some complained that the highly experienced foreign aid workers were being prevented from traveling to Palu.
According to the Indonesian regulation, financing, supplies and foreign staff can only have access to the site if it is declared a "national disaster zone".
That hasn't happened yet.
“It’s still at a level of provincial catastrophe,” said Aulia Arriani, a spokeswoman for the Indonesian Red Cross.
"As soon as the government says "OK, this is a national catastrophe" we can open the doors to international assistance, but that statute doesn't exist yet."
At the fall of the second night in Palu after Friday's quake and tsunami, friends and family of those still missing still maintained the hope that their loved ones would be part of the miracles that mitigate the grim headlines of natural catastrophes.
On Saturday, a boy was pulled out of a sewer.
On Sunday, rescuers released a woman who had been trapped in the rubble for two days beside her mother's body.
Gendon Subandono, coach of paragliding Indonesia's national team, had coached two of his missing sportsmen for the Asian Games, completed earlier this month in Indonesia.
Others of those imprisoned at the Hotel Roa Roa, including Mr. Mandagi, were his students.
“As a senior paragliding practitioner, I have to carry my own emotional burden,” he said.
Mr. Gendon recounted that in the hours after news of the collapse of the Hotel Roa Roa began circulating among the paragliding community, he desperately sent messages on WhatsApp to Palu’s sportsmen, who were participating in the festival on the beach.
However, their messages produced only one grey checking mark, rather than a pair of blues.
"I think that means the messages haven't been delivered," he said.
