Kanye West: Rapper changes his name to Ye
Rapper Kanye West changed his name to Ye.
Announcing this change on Twitter on Saturday, he wrote: "The being previously known as Kanye West."
West, aged 41, received some time ago Ye's nickname and used it as the title of his eighth album, released in June.
The change comes ahead of his participation in Saturday Night Live, where he is expected to present his new Yandhi album.
This intervention is intended to replace that of singer Ariana Grande, who canceled it for "emotional motives", reported the creator of the show.
Being at the same time an abbreviation of his current professional name, the word has for him a religious meaning, according to West.
"I think "ye" is the most commonly used word in the Bible, and in the Bible it means "you," West said earlier this year, when he commented on the title of his album with radio presenter Big Boy.
"Therefore, I am you, I am us, it's us.
It comes from Kanye, which means "the only one," to just Ye - being just a reflection of our good, of our evil, of our confusion, of everything.
The album is another reflection of who we are.”
Kanye is part of a series of famous rappers who have changed their names.
Sean Combs was alternately known as Puff Daddy, P. Diddy or Diddy, but this year announced his preference for the names Love and Brother Love.
Former West collaborator, JAY-Z, also used variations of his name with or without hyphen and uppercases.
In Mexico, AMLO undertakes not to resort to military against civilians
Mexican President-elect Andres Manuel López Obrador has pledged not to use military force against civilians, at a time when the country approaches the 50th anniversary of a bloody reprisal against students.
López Obrador vowed on Saturday at Tlatelolco Plaza "never using the army to repress the Mexican people."
Military personnel fired at a peaceful demonstration in the square on October 2, 1968, resulting in a number of deaths that may have amounted to 300, when leftist student movements were deployed throughout Latin America.
