Duplicating funding could double the chances that the next great success of AI will be achieved by the United States, Lee told NBC in an interview this week.
Lee, whose book "AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order" was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, is the CEO of Sinovation Ventures, a company that has invested in one of the most prominent AI companies in China, Face++.
In the 1980’s at Carnegie Mellon University, he worked on an AI system that defeated Othello’s highest-ranked American player. He later held executive roles at Microsoft Research and was president of Google’s branch in China.
Lee credited the U.S. government's previous technology contests as the Defense Department Agency's Robotics Challenge for Advanced Investigation Projects and asked when it was going to be the next, with a view to helping identify the next visionaries.
U.S. investigators often have to make a big effort to obtain state funding, Lee said.
