Macedonians vote in referendum on changing the country's name
The Macedonians went to votes on Sunday to decide on changing the country's name to "Republic of North Macedonia," an initiative that would resolve a decades-long dispute with Greece that blocked its calls for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which is part of a province called Macedonia, insists that its northern neighbour's name represents a complaint about its territory and has vetoed its entry into NATO and the EU.
The two governments signed an agreement in June based on the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents argue that the move would be a blow to the ethnic identity of the mostly Slavic Macedonian population.
President Gjorge Ivanov has stated that he will not vote in the referendum and a boycott campaign has raised doubts as to whether turnout will be enough to meet the 50% needed for the referendum to be valid.
The question of the referendum was as follows: "Is it in favour of entry into NATO and the EU with the acceptance of the agreement with Greece?"
Supporters of the name change, which includes Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that it is a price worth paying to pursue admission efforts at bodies with the EU and NATO to Macedonia, one of the countries that emerged from the collapse of Yugoslavia.
"I have come today to vote for the future of our country, for the young people of Macedonia, so that they can live free under the auspices of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us," said Olivera Georgijevska, 79, in Skopje.
Although the referendum is not legally binding, a sufficient number of members of parliament stated that it would respect the outcome of the vote to make it decisive.
The name change would require a two-thirds majority in parliament.
The national election commission stated that there had been no records of irregularities until one in the afternoon.
However, the turnout was only 16%, compared with the 34% obtained in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66% of registered voters appeared to vote.
"I've come to vote for my children; our place is in Europe," said Gjose Tanevski, 62, a voter from the capital, Skopje.
The Prime Minister of Macedonia, Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and son Dushko vote in the referendum on the change of the country's name, which would open the doors to its accession to NATO and the European Union in Strumica, Macedonia, on September 30, 2018.
In front of parliament in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, prepared a small stage and placed chairs in front of the tents set up by those who will boycott the referendum.
“We are for NATO and the EU, but we want to join upright and not through the service door,” Kavadarkov said.
"We're a poor country, but we have dignity.
If you don’t want to welcome us as Macedonia, we can turn to others like China and Russia to become part of Eurasian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaev said entry into NATO would bring much-needed investments to Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of more than 20%.
"I believe that the overwhelming majority will be in favour, because more than 80% of our citizens are in favour of the EU and NATO," Zaev said.
According to him, a “yes” would be the “confirmation of our future.”
A poll published last Monday by Macedonia's Political Research Institute revealed that between 30 and 43% of voters would take part in the referendum, lower than the affluence required.
Another poll, conducted by Telma TV in Macedonia, found that 57% of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
Of those, 70 percent said they'd vote yes.
For the success of the referendum, the turnout has to be more than 50% plus one vote.
The failure of the referendum would represent the first major failure in pro-Western government policy since it took office in May last year.
