Labour has ceased to be inclusive.
It is intolerant of those who openly give their opinion
When Momentum activists from my local party organisation voted to censure me, it wasn't a surprise to me.
After all, I'm the latest in a number of Labour MPs in parliament who have been told they aren't welcome - all for openly expressing our views.
My colleague in parliament Joan Ryan was the subject of similar treatment because she resolutely faced anti-Semitism.
In my case, the censure motion criticized me for disagreeing with Jeremy Corbyn.
On the importance of a responsible economic policy on national security on Europe, ironically similar issues on which Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
The notification for the Nottingham East Labour meeting on Friday mentioned that "we intend the meetings to be inclusive and productive."
For most of my eight years as a local Labour representative in parliament, General Committee meetings were exactly that.
Unfortunately, today, it has ceased to be the tone of many of the meetings and the promise of "moster and kinder" politics has long been forgotten, if ever to come true.
It has become increasingly apparent that different views are not tolerated in the Labour Party, and that every opinion is judged on whether or not it is acceptable by the party leadership.
This began shortly after Jeremy took the lead, because the colleagues with whom he thought he shared a similar political perspective began to look forward to a turnaround from me and to take positions with which I would never agree - whether they were about national security or the EU single market.
Whenever I speak in public (and it doesn't really matter what I'm saying), there follows a torrent of insults on social networks that call for "dismission", denouncing the policies of the center, telling me that I should not belong to the Labor Party.
And it's not just me who's going through this.
In fact, I know that I am luckier than some of my colleagues, since the comments addressed to me tend to be of a political character.
I am amazed by the professionalism and determination of those colleagues who face a barrage of sexist or racist insults every day, but who never retreat before them.
One of the aspects that disappoints me the most in this era of politics is the way these levels of insult have become normalised.
Jeremy Corbyn claimed last week that Labour should promote a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we have ceased to be inclusive, and with every "censorship" motion or amendment of the selection rules the party narrows its views.
I've received countless pieces of advice over the last two years urging me to keep my head down, not to be so expressive, and so "it'd be okay" for me.
But that's not why I came to do politics.
Since I joined the Labour Party 32 years ago as a student, driven by the negligence of Thatcher's government that left the classroom of my comprehensive school literally in rubble, I sought to fight for better public services for those who need them most, either as a mayor or as a minister in the government.
I've never hidden my policies, including in the last election.
No one in Nottingham East could have been somehow confused as to my political positions and areas of disagreement with the current leadership.
For those promoting the motion on Friday, all I have to say is that when the country is heading towards a Brexit that will harm families, businesses and our public services, I don’t understand the desire to waste time and energy on the subject of my loyalty to the Labour leader.
But really, the message I have is not for Nottingham's Momentum; it's for my constituents, members of the Labour Party or not: I'm proud to serve you, and I promise that no threats of "redundancy" or political expedients will prevent me from acting in the sense of what I believe to be the interests of all of you.
Chris Leslie is an MP in parliament for Nottingham East
Ayr 38 - 17 Melrose: the unbeatable Ayr goes to the top
Two late rehearsals have somehow biased the result, but there's no doubt that Ayr deserved to triumph in this incredibly interesting Tennent's Premiership game of the day.
The team is now at the top of the table, the only one of ten without defeats.
In the end, it was superior defence and a better management of the risks that made the home team lead to the best, and coach Peter Murchie had every reason to be satisfied.
“We’ve been tested in our games so far and we’re still unbeatable, so I have to be happy,” he said.
Robyn Christie of Melrose said: "Merit goes to Ayr. They've managed risks better than us."
Grant Anderson's 14th-minute rehearsal, converted by Frazier Climo, put Ayr at the forefront, but a yellow card for Scottish captain Rory Hughes, ceded for the game by the Warriors, allowed Melrose to give expression to the numbers, Jason Baggot taking advantage of an unconverted rehearsal.
Climo extended Ayr's advantage with a big penalty, before, immediately before mid-time had scored and then converted a solo rehearsal to put the marker at 17-5 to Ayr at halftime.
However, Melrose began the second part well and Patrick Anderson's rehearsal, converted by Baggot, reduced the disadvantage to five points.
There followed a lengthy interruption due to a serious injury from Ruaridh Knott, which was transported from stretcher to the outside and, after the restart, Ayr reinforced the lead with a rehearsal of Stafford McDowall converted by Climo.
Ayr's acting captain Blair Macpherson then received a yellow card and, once again, Melrose took advantage of the numerical advantage with an unconverted rehearsal of Bruce Colvine at the end of an intense pressure onslaught.
However, the home team returned, and when Struan Hutchinson received a yellow card for having knocked Climo without the ball, on the penalty throw, MacPherson put the ball on the ground behind the maul in Ayr's progress.
Climo converted, as he had done almost immediately after the restart, after Kyle Rowe had collected David Armstrong's box kick and sent winger Gregor Henry to the fifth rehearsal of the home team.
Still Game star ready for a new career in restoration business
Ford Kieran, star of the Still Game, seems ready to enter the restoration sector after it is known that he has been appointed director of a licensed restoration company.
The 56-year-old actor represents Jack Jarvis in the popular BBC series, which writes and participates with long-time comedian partner Greg Hemphill.
The pair have announced that the ninth season, to premiere soon, will be the last in the series, and it looks like Kiernan is planning his life after Craiglang.
According to the official records, he's director of Adriftmorn Limited.
The actor declined to comment on the story, even though a Scottish Sun source suggested that Kiernan was intending to get involved in the "florescent Glasgow restoration business."
"The sea is ours": Bolivia, a country devoid of coastline, expects the court to re-embrace a path to the Pacific
Sailors patrol a rope-covered naval base at La Paz.
Public buildings sport a navy-blue flag.
Lake Titicaca's naval bases for the Amazon bear the motto: "The sea is ours by right.
Recovering it is a duty.”
Throughout Bolivia, a landlocked country, the memory of a coastline lost to Chile is still alive in a bloody conflict for resources in the 19th century, as well as the longing to sail once again in the Pacific Ocean.
Those hopes are perhaps at the highest point of decades, since Bolivia awaits a sentence from the International Court of Justice on October 1, after five years of deliberations.
"Bolivolia has the impetus, a spirit of unity and serenity and, of course, a positive outlook on the result," said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will witness the utterance of IJT’s sentence on giant screens scattered throughout the country, hopeful that the Hague court will deliberate in favor of their country’s complaint, so that, after decades of uncertain negotiations, Chile will be obliged to negotiate, granting Bolivia a sovereign exit to the sea.
Evo Morales, the charismatic Bolivian indigenous president, who faces a controversial battle for re-election next year, also has a lot at stake in Monday's sentence.
"We are very close to returning to the Pacific Ocean," were the president's votes last August.
However, some analysts believe it is unlikely that the court will deliberate in favor of Bolivia, and that little would change even otherwise.
The U.N. body, headquartered in the Netherlands, does not have the power to assign Chilean territory to another country and has stipulated that it will not determine the outcome of possible negotiations.
That IJT's sentence be handed down just six months after the hearing of the closing allegations indicates that the case "was not complicated," said Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert on international law.
And far from promoting the Bolivian cause, the last four years may have harmed it.
"The issue of access to the sea has been hijacked by the current Bolivian administration," Zárate said.
Morales' belligerent rhetoric undermined some Chilean residual goodwill, he suggested.
At a time in the future, Bolivia and Chile will continue the talks, but it will be extremely difficult to maintain the dialogue after this.
The two countries haven't exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, Bolivia’s representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court’s decision-making process was unusually quick.
Next Monday will give Bolivia "an extraordinary opportunity to initiate a new era of relations with Chile" and a possibility of "putting an end to 139 years of disagreements with mutual benefits," he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales, still one of the most popular presidents in Latin America, was using the maritime issue as a political crutch.
"Bolivia will never dispense with its right of access to the Pacific Ocean," he added.
“Sentencing is an opportunity to understand that we must overcome the past.”
North Korea says there will be no disarmament without the country trusting the U.S
North Korea's Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho said his nation would never dispense with its nuclear weapons if it did not rely on Washington first.
Ri spoke Saturday at the United Nations General Assembly.
In his speech, he urged the United States to deliver on the promises it made during a summit in Singapore between the leaders of rival countries.
His comments come as U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears about to restart nuclear diplomacy at stalement, more than three months after the Singapore summit with North Korean President Kim Jong Un.
Ri mentioned that it is an "impossible dream" to consider that the continuing sanctions and U.S. objection to a declaration that puts an end to the Korean war will ever lead the North to kneel.
Washington fears accepting the statement without Pyongyang first taking significant steps towards disarmament.
Both Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump intend a second summit.
However, there is widespread skepticism that Pyongyang really intends to reduce an arsenal that the country probably sees as the only way to guarantee its security.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to prepare a second Kim-Trump summit.
Fashion shows in Paris reveal the latest line of huge hats on their way to a store near themselves
If you want to widen your collection of hats or fully protect yourself from the sun, you don’t have to look any further.
Designers Valentino and Thom Browne presented on the catwalk a series of extravagant and oversized hats for their spring-summer 2019 collections, which amazed the style set at Paris Fashion Week.
This summer, Instagram was flooded with very unpractical hats and these designers sent these amazing creations to the catwalk.
Valentino's most outstanding piece was an extravagant beige hat adorned with a feather-like wide flap that flooded the models' heads.
Other oversized accessories included creved jelly watermelons, a wizard's hat and even a pineapple, although they weren't designed to keep their heads warm.
Thom Browne also presented a selection of bizarre masks...even in time for the carnival.
Many of the colourful masks had cooked lips and looked more like Hannibal Lecter than haute couture.
One of the creations resembled diving equipment, with tube and glasses, while another looked like a molten ice cream cone.
And if you want to continue this immense fashion statement, you're lucky.
Stylism observers predict that these huge hats are on their way to a store near themselves.
Oversized hats appear effervescent on the path of "La Bomba," the straw hat with a 61 cm flap that's been worn by everyone from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The brand of worship behind the very unpractical hat that spread through social media sent another great creation to the catwalks: a beach bag in straw almost as large as the bathing model carrying it.
The burnt-orange raffia bag, adorned with raffia fringes and with a white leather handle, was the most outstanding piece of Jacquemus' Spring-Summer La Riviera collection at Paris Fashion Week.
Famed designer Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: "I'm waiting to see large hats and beach bags arrive in stores for next summer. Since the designer has had so much impact, it would be hard to ignore the search for oversized accessories."
John Edward: language skills are essential for global citizens
Scotland’s independent schools benefit from a curriculum of academic excellence, and this trend continued in 2018 with another set of exceptional exam results, reinforced by individual and collective success in sport, art, music and other community initiatives.
With more than 30 000 pupils spread throughout Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), strive to obtain the best level of service for both students and parents.
Independent schools intend to prepare their pupils for deepening their studies and access to universities, as well as for the career they have chosen and to take their place as global citizens.
Since this is an educational sector capable of devising and implementing a tailor-made school curriculum, we see that modern languages continue to be a popular and sought-after topic in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: “If we talk to a man in a language that he understands, we can get into his mind.
If we talk to him in his own language, we can get into his heart.”
This quote reminds us well that we cannot only depend on English when we want to create relationships and trust with people from other countries.
Based on the results of this year’s recent exams, we found that languages are at the top of the table, with the highest rates of use of independent schools.
A total of 68% of students who studied foreign languages reached the maximum rating of A.
These data, collected in the 74 schools under the scope of SCIS, show that 72% of students obtained the maximum grade of A in Mandarin, while 72% of those who studied German, 69% of those who studied French and 63% of those who studied Spanish also obtained an A.
This demonstrates that Scottish independent schools are favouring foreign languages as vital skills that will unquestionably serve children and young people in the future.
Languages, as an option, are now viewed in the same way as STEM (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the curricula of independent schools and not only.
A survey by the United Kingdom Commission on Employment and Competencies in 2014 concluded that, among the reasons for employers' difficulty in filling vacancies, 17% concerned the scarcity of language skills.
Therefore, knowing other languages is increasingly an imperative to prepare the young generation for their future careers.
With more potential employment opportunities requiring knowledge of languages, these skills are essential in a globalised world.
Regardless of the career chosen, if they have learned a second language, they will have a tangible advantage in the future with an aptitude for life like this.
The ability to communicate directly with people from foreign countries will automatically put a multilingual person ahead of the competition.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4000 adults in the United Kingdom in 2013, 75% did not speak a foreign language well enough to maintain a conversation, French being the only language spoken by a two-digit proportion, 15%.
Therefore, investing now in language teaching is important for today’s children.
Mastery of several languages, particularly those of developing countries, will give children with the best chance of finding employment with relevance.
In Scotland, every school differs in the languages it teaches.
Some of them will focus on the most classical modern languages, while others will teach languages considered the most important for the United Kingdom for the 2020 horizon, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever the interest of your child, there will always be a set of languages to choose from in independent schools, supported by specialized teaching staff in the area.
Scottish independent schools are committed to providing a pedagogical environment that prepares children and gives them the necessary skills for success, whatever the future holds.
It is undeniable that today, in a globalized business environment, languages continue to be of fundamental importance for the country’s future. Therefore, it is necessary to reflect it on education.
In fact, modern languages should be considered "aptitudes of international communication."
Independent schools will continue to provide this option with diversity and excellence for young Scots.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is the director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
LeBron kicks off the Lakers on Sunday in San Diego
The wait is ending for fans looking to see LeBron James kick off at the Los Angeles Lakers.
Luke Walton, coach of the Lakers, has announced that James will take part in Sunday's pre-season opening game against the Denver Nuggets in San Diego.
However, how many minutes you will play is yet undetermined.
“It will be more than one and fewer than 48,” Walton said on the Lakers’ official website.
Lakers journalist Mike Trudell posted a tweet stating that it's likely that James plays a limited number of minutes.
After practice earlier this week, James was asked about his plans for the Lakers' six-game schedule in the pre-season.
"I don't need pre-season games at this stage of my career to be ready," he said.
Time and YouTube Channel of the Trump Rally in West Virginia
President Donald Trump kicks off a round of campaign rallies tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
It's the first of five rallies scheduled for next week, which include stops at friendly venues like Tennessee and Mississippi.
With the conformation vote on standby for his pick for the Supreme Court vacancy, Trump intends to bolster support for the midterm elections to be held soon as Republicans are at risk of losing control of Congress after November's vote.
What time is the Trump rally in West Virginia tonight and how to watch it online?
Trump's rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for 7 p.m., Eastern Hour, on Saturday, September 29, 2018.
You'll be able to watch Trump's rally in West Virginia online below in a live stream on YouTube.
Trump will likely address this week’s hearings to Brett Kavanaugh, appointed to the Supreme Court, who have become strained due to allegations of sexual abuse. The confirmation vote envisioned in the Senate will be on standby for a week while the FBI proceeds to investigations.
However, the main goal of this torrent of rallies is to help Republicans, who face tough elections in November, gain some momentum.
Accordingly, officials of President Trump’s campaign had stated that these five rallies next week intend “to give energy to volunteers and supporters at a time when Republicans try to protect and extend the majorities they hold in the Senate and the House of Representatives.”
"Congressional control is just as fundamental to the president's agenda that he will visit as many states as possible as we get closer to the campaign stive," a Trump campaign spokesman who resigned was quoted as saying.
Marked for Wesbanco Arena in Wheeling, tonight’s rally could lure supporters “from Ohio to Pennsylvania and gain media coverage from the Pittsburgh area,” according to West Virginia Metro News.
Saturday will mark the second time this month that Trump visits West Virginia, the state in which he won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump is trying to help Republican West Virginia Senate candidate Patrick Morrisey at a disadvantage in the polls.
“It’s not a good sign for Morrisey that the president will have to come to help him in the polls,” Simon Haeder, a political scientist at the University of West Virginia, told Reuters.
Ryder Cup 2018: U.S. team demonstrates they have the strength to fight to keep hope alive before Sunday's singles
After three uniform sessions, Saturday afternoon foursomes may be exactly what this Ryder Cup needed.
Pendular oscillation of momentum is a fully invented sports concept, but it is one in which players truly believe, and never more than in competitions like these.
So where would they say momentum will come now?
"They had a six-point lead and now it's four, so we're looking at it as a bit of momentum, I'd say," Jordan Spieth said as he was leaving the field at the end of the day.
Europe is at an advantage, of course, with four points ahead and twelve more at stake.
Americans, as Spieth claims, still have some reserve energy and plenty of reasons to feel encouraged, especially given the form of Spieth and Justin Thomas, who played together all day and each of them got three points out of four.
Spieth has been deadly from tee to green and is leading by example.
Those guttural shouts of festion were becoming taller as the round proceeded, with a crucial putt that made the fourth match an all-square when he and Thomas were two holes behind in two.
His putt who won the match No. 15 produced in a similar cry, of the kind that reveals that he still believes that the American team is still in the competition.
“We just have to focus and worry about our own game,” Spieth said.
That's all that's left for each of these players.
18 holes to make a mark.
The only players with more points than Spieth and Thomas in the last two days are Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood, the undisputed revelation of the Ryder Cup.
The European pair, improbable but adorable, are four out of four and can't get it wrong.
"Moliwood" was the only duet not to do a bogey on Saturday afternoon, but also avoided bogeys on Saturday morning, Friday afternoon and on Friday's nine back in the morning.
In this series, and given the way their energy seems to flow from and to this turbulent crowd, one consolidates the idea that they are the players beating on Sunday, and that there is no more popular player to seal a potential European sunshit victory at Le Golf National than Fleetwood or Molinari.
Preferably both simultaneously in different holes.
However, the discourse on the glory of Europe remains premature.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson defeated Sergio Garcia, this morning's fourball hero, when teamed with Alex Noren.
One bogey and two doubles on the front nine shoved the Spaniard and Swede into a hole that they were never close to leaving.
However, on Sunday, there's no one who helps them get out of it.
Fourballs and foursomes are so fascinating to see closely due to the interactions between the partners, the advice they give or not, and how the strategy can change in an instant.
Europe has played better as a team so far and acquired a significant advantage on the final day. However, this foursomes session also demonstrated that the U.S. team has the stomach for the fight that some, especially in the United States, doubted they had.
Europe with a 10-6 lead on the last day of the Ryder Cup
Europe will enter the last day of the Ryder Cup with a slack lead after it came out of fourballs and foursomes of Saturday's matches with a 10-6 lead against the United States.
The inspired duet of Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari led the load with two wins over a Tiger Woods struggling to get to qualifying at Le Golf National, so far, four-point.
Thomas Bjorn's European team, struggling to conserve the trophy he lost at Hazeltine two years ago, dominated an unsuccessful American team in the fourballs of the morning, winning the series 3-1.
The U.S. offered more resistance in the foursomes with two matches won, but failed to shorten the disadvantage.
Jim Furyk's team needs eight points on Sunday's 12th singles to conserve the trophy.
Fleetwood is the first European beginner to win four points in a row when he and Molinari, who were nicknamed "Molliwood," after a sensational weekend are just the only duet to win four points in their four opening matches in Ryder Cup history.
Having crushed Woods and Patrick Reed in the fourballs, they superbly cemented the advantage by defeating a powerless Woods and American beginner Bryson Dechambeau with an even more emphatic 5&4.
Woods, who dragged off during two matches on Saturday, revealed occasional hits of genius, but has already lost 19 of the matches he played in fourballs and foursomes, and seven in a row.
Justin Rose, resting during the morning fourballs, returned to partner Henrik Stenson in the foursomes, defeating 2&1 Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, first and third in the world rankings.
However, on this pleasant day with a light breeze southwest of Paris, Europe has not always led to the best.
Jordan Spieth, a three-time winner of major men's tournaments, and Justin Thomas set the benchmark for Americans with two points on Saturday.
The duet scored a brave 2&1 win over Jon Rahm and Ian Poulter of Spain and later returned to beat Poulter and Rory McIlroy by 4&3 in the foursomes, having lost both opening holes.
Only twice in Ryder Cup history did a team reverse a four-point handicap before the singles, although the title-holding Furyk team only need to draw to conserve the trophy.
After two days in second position, a counter-attack on Sunday seems to be beyond its possibilities.
North Korea says "neither thinking" to unilateral disarmament without confidence
North Korea’s foreign minister told the United Nations on Saturday that the continuation of sanctions was aggravating distrust of the United States and that there was no way for the country to unilaterally dispense with its nuclear weapons in those circumstances.
Ri Yong Ho told the annual General Assembly of the world body that North Korea had taken "significant goodwill measures" last year, such as stopping nuclear and missile tests, dismantling the nuclear testing center and a commitment to non-proliferation of nuclear weapons and technologies.
“However, we don’t see any corresponding U.S. response,” he said.
“Without trust in the U.S. there will be no confidence in our national security and in such circumstances there is no way for us to proceed to our disarmament first.”
Although Ri has recycled well-known North Korean complaints about Washington’s resistance to a “phased” approach to denuclearization, under which North Korea would be rewarded as it carried out gradual steps, its statements appear to be significant in that they do not liminally reject unilateral denuclearization, in contrast to Pyongyang’s previous positions.
Ri referred to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the first summit ever held between an acting U.S. president and a North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, where Kim pledged to work towards "denuclearization of the Korean peninsula," while Trump offered security guarantees to North Korea.
North Korea has sought to achieve a formal end to Korea's 1950-53 war, but the United States claims Pyongyang must first abandon its nuclear weapons.
Washington has also resisted calls to soften tough international sanctions against North Korea.
"The United States insists on "denuclearization first" and increases the level of pressure with sanctions to achieve their coercive goal, opposing even the "declaration of the end of the war," Ri said.
"The perception that sanctions can bring us to our knees is an impossible dream of people who know nothing about us.
However, the problem is that the prolongation of sanctions is aggravating our distrust."
Ri did not mention plans for the second summit between Kim and Trump that had been referred to by the U.S. leader at the United Nations earlier in the week.
Instead, the minister highlighted the three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the past five months, having added: "If the interested party on this issue were South Korea and not the U.S., the denuclearization of the Korean peninsula would not have reached this impasse."
Still, the tone of Ri’s speech was drastically different from last year, when he told the U.N. General Assembly that guiding North Korea’s rockets into U.S
This year at the United Nations, Trump, who last year had threatened to “completely destroy” North Korea, filled Kim with praise for his courage to take steps towards disarmament, but stated that there was still much to be done and that sanctions would have to continue until the denuclearization of North Korea.
On Wednesday, Trump claimed he didn't have a timetable for it, stating that "whether it takes two years, three years or five months, it doesn't matter."
China and Russia argue that the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the steps it has taken.
U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: "Application of Security Council sanctions must proceed vigorously until denuclearization is total, final and verifiable."
The Security Council has unanimously tightened sanctions against North Korea imposed since 2006 in an attempt to strangle North Korea's nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo meeted Ri on the sidelines of the U.N. General Assembly and declared afterwards that he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for a second summit.
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but the last visit didn't go well.
He left Pyongyang in July saying progress had been made, although later North Korea denounced it for "making gangster demands.
At a meeting with Moon this month, North Korea pledged to dismantle a missile facility and also a nuclear complex if the United States "took correspondent action."
Pompeo said Kim had told him that the “corresponding measures” they were looking for were Trump’s security guarantees in Singapore, as well as initiatives toward normalizing relations with Washington.
Harvard students attend course that teaches them how to rest enough
A new course at Harvard University this year has put all its students to sleep more in an initiative to combat the macho culture of studying in caffeine-pivoted "directs."
One professor concluded that students at the top of the world’s rankings didn’t often have the slightest idea of the most basic concepts about the care they should have with themselves.
Charles Czeisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and an expert at Brigham and Women's Hospital, was the author of the course, which he believes to be the first of its kind in the U.S.
The inspiration for the course arose after giving a lecture on the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
"In the end, a girl came to me and said, "Why are they just telling me this in the last year of the course?"
“The young woman stated that no one has ever mentioned the importance of sleep, which surprised me.”
The course, taught for the first time this year, explains to students the essential concepts about how good sleep habits contribute positively to academic and athletic performance, as well as to the improvement of overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's health services, stated that the institution decided to introduce the course after concluding that some students suffered from severe sleep deprivation during the week.
The one-hour course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In a section there is an image of a dormitory where students click on coffee mugs, curtains, sneakers and books to be informed about the effects of caffeine and light, and on the way athletic performance is influenced by sleep deficiency, and the importance of a routine to collect to bed.
In another section, participants are informed about how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risks of stroke, depression and cancer.
Then, a campus map, with interactive icons, encourages participants to think about their daily routines.
“We don’t know that it won’t change students’ behavior instantly.
However, we believe that they have the right to know, just as we all have the right to know the health effects of the smoking option," Professor Czeisler added.
Pride's culture in "making direct" still exists, it said, adding that modern technology and increasing pressure on students makes this a growing problem.
Ensuring that we get enough and with quality should be a “secret weapon” of the students to combat stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he said, and even to avoid getting fat, because sleep deprivation puts the brain in a hungry way, making them constantly starving.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old Californian student in physical biology and chemistry, helped Professor Czeisler conceive the course after attending one of his classes last year the first year at Harvard.
The course opened his eyes and inspired him to encourage the creation of a campus-wide course, he said.
The next step, according to their expectations, is to ask all graduate students to attend a similar study program before entering the competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler recommended that students consider the use of an alarm for the time to go to bed, as well as to wake up, and to be aware of the harmful effects of the "blue light" emitted by electronic screens and LED lighting, which can unregulate the circadian rhythm and lead to problems falling asleep.
Livingston 1 - Rangers 0: Menga's goal knocks Gerrard's men
Rangers suffered another access of sadness away from home when Dolly Menga's attack caused Steven Gerrard's disjointed team a 1-0 defeat at Livingston's stadium.
Ibrox's team was looking to record their first win in their course since the 1-0 triumph at St Johnstone, but Gary Holt's team eventually inflicted Gerrard's second defeat in 18 games, leaving his team eight points behind unbridled Hearts, the Scottish first division leader.
Menga scored seven minutes before the end of the first half and Rangers collective, devoid of inspiration, never seemed to be able to match.
With Rangers now descending to sixth, Livingston rise to third, and only behind Hibernian for the goal difference.
And even more problems may arise for the Rangers after the Calum Spence line supervisor received treatment of a head injury caused by an object apparently thrown from the visitor's area.
Gerrard made eight changes to the team that surpassed Ayr in Betfred Cup semi-finals.
Holt, for his part, used the same eleven of Livingston who stole a point from Hearts last week, and will have been delighted with how his well-oiled team smothered opponents on each move.
Rangers may have mastered possession, but Livingston did more with the one they had.
The team could have scored as early as two minutes, when the first pass sent Scott Pittman to Allan McGregor's beacon, but the midfielder wasted his great opportunity out.
A long free kick from Keaghan Jacobs went to meet captain Craig Halkett, but his defensive partner Alan Lithgow managed only to shoot out towards the second beam.
Rangers managed to regain control, but hope was greater than the belief in their game in the final third.
Certainly Alfredo Morelos thought he should have been awarded a penalty to the quarter-hour with his collision with Steven Lawless, but referee Steven Thomson refused the Colombian's appeals.
Rangers managed just two shots on goal in the first half, but former Ibrox goalkeeper Liam Kelly had little to worry about Lassana Coulibaly's head shot and Ovie Ejaria's weak shot.
Although Livingston's 34th-minute goal may have been contrary to what was expected of the development of the game, it is undeniable that the team deserved it, let alone for its toughness.
Once again, the Rangers couldn't cope with a long stalled ball play from Jacobs.
Scott Arfield didn't react when Declan Gallagher made a pass for Scott Robinson, who kept calm when he chose Menga for a simple finish.
Gerrard moved at halftime, having replaced Coulibaly by Ryan Kent, and this exchange almost had an immediate impact when the side striker moved on to Morelos, but the impressive Kelly made a sprint from his line to effect the lockdown.
However, Livingston continued to compel visitors to play exactly the kind of game they like, with Lithgow and Halkett taking a long pass after a long pass.
Holt's team may have increased their advantage in the final stages, but McGregor held up well, defending himself from Jacobs before Lithgow shot out in the corner.
Rangers replacement Glenn Middleton again complained about a big penalty when he tangled at Jacobs, but Thomson once again ignored the request.
Almanac: the inventor of the Geiger accountant
And now a page of our Almanac "Sunday Morning": On September 30, 1882, 136 years ago and continues to COUNT ... the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the device known as Geiger counter.
A fundamental piece of science since then, accountant Geiger has also become a central element of pop culture, such as in the 1950 film "Bells of Coronado" in which the improbable cowboy scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans participated:
Man: "What the hell is that?"
Rogers: "It's a Geiger counter, used to locate radioactive minerals as a uranium.
When we place these headphones, we can even hear the effects of atoms released by radioactivity on minerals.”
Evans: "Look, it's really crackling!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention to which it gave the name still endures.
New Cancer Vaccine Teaches Immune System to "See" Harmful Cells
A new vaccine for cancer teaches the immune system to "see" harmful cells and kill them
The vaccine teaches the immune system how to recognize harmful cells as part of treatment
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from a patient and their alteration in the laboratory
These cells, which can "see" a protein common to many cancers, are then injected again into the body
An experimental vaccine is yielding promising results in patients with a number of cancers.
A woman treated with the vaccine, who teaches the immune system to recognize harmful cells, has seen her ovarian cancer disappear for more than 18 months.
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from a patient and their alteration in the laboratory so that they can "see" a protein common to many cancers, called HER2. These cells are then again injected into the body.
Professor Jay Berzofsky, from the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland, stated, "Our results suggest that we have a very promising vaccine."
HER2 "controls the growth of various types of cancer," including those of the breast, the ovary, the lungs and also colorectals, Professor Berzofsky explained.
An approach similar to that involving removing immune cells from patients and "teaching" them to attack cancer cells worked in the treatment of a type of leukemia.
Kanye West embarks on a diatribe in support of Trump wearing a MAGA hat following his appearance at the SNL.
It didn't go well
Kanye West was booed in the studio on Saturday Night Live after an incoherent performance in which he praised U.S. President Donald Trump, stating that he would run for presidential elections in 2020.
After performing his song of the night, named Ghost Town, in which he wore a "Make America Great" hat, he started a bravada against the Democrats and reiterated his support for Trump.
"So many times I talk to a white man and he says to me, "How can you like Trump? He's racist!"
Well, if I had been concerned about racism, I would've abandoned America a long time ago," he said.
The SNL began with a satire with Matt Damon, in which the Hollywood star mocked Brett Kavanaugh's testimony before the Senate Judiciary Committee about the sexual assault allegations made by Christine Blasey Ford.
Although it hadn't been broadcast, West's bravado scene was posted on social media by comedian Chris Rock.
It's not clear if Rock was trying to mock West when he did it.
In addition, West had complained to the public that he had had a hard time behind the scenes because of the hat.
"They intimidated me behind the scenes.
They told me, "Don't go there in that hat."
They intimidated me!
And then they tell me that I'm marginalized," he said, according to the Washington Examiner.
West continued: "Want to see the marginalization?", saying that "I'd put on my superman cover, because that means no one tells me what to do. Do they want the world to move forward?
Try love.”
His comments provoked booing from the public and SNL participants appeared to be embarrassed, Variety reported, with someone from the show told the newspaper: "The whole studio was completely silent."
West had been invited to replace singer Ariana Grande, whose former boyfriend, rapper Mac Miller, had passed away a few days ago.
West mistook many for performing the song I Love it disguised as Perrier's bottle.
West received a rally of support from Conservative group director TPUSA Candace Turner, who posted the tweet: "To one of our bravest spirits: THANK YOU FOR FACINGING TURBA."
However, interview show host Karen Hunter posted a tweet saying that West was simply "being what it is, and that's absolutely wonderful."
"But I've chosen NOT to reward someone (buying their music or supporting their "art") that I believe is welcoming and propagating ideologies harmful to the community.
He's free.
And so do we," he added.
Prior to the show, the rapper announced on Twitter that he had changed his name, stating that it was now "the being previously known as Kanye West."
West isn't the first artist to change his name, being followed by Diddy's passes, also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
Rapper Snoop Dogg has used the name Snoop Lion and, of course, the missing musical legend Prince changed the name to a symbol and then to "the artist previously known as Prince."
Accusation of attempted murder after stabbing at Belfast restaurant
A 45-year-old man has been charged with attempted murder after a man was stabbed in a restaurant in eastern Belfast on Friday.
The incident took place in Ballyhackamore, police said.
The defendant is expected to be presented by the Belfast Court on Monday.
The accusations will be reviewed by the public ministry.
Throne Star Kit Harington attacks toxic masculinity
Kit Harington is known for his fencing role as Jon Snow in HBO's violent medieval fantasy series, Game of Thrones.
However, the 31-year-old actor attacked the stereotype of the macho hero, stating that such roles on screen make guys often feel like they have to be tough to be respected.
In a conversation with Sunday Times Culture, Kit said he believes "something went wrong" and wondered how to address the problem of toxic masculinity in the #MeToo era.
Kit, who recently married his colleague in the 31-year-old Rose Leslie Throne War, admitted that he has a "quiet position" on addressing the problem.
"I have a very strong personal position right now: what went wrong with masculinity?" he said.
"What have we been teaching men when they're growing up in terms of the problem we've seen right now?"
Kit believes that television may be partly responsible for the rise of toxic masculinity due to its very masculine characters.
“What is innate and what is taught?
What is taught on TV and on the streets that makes young people feel they have to belong to this particular form of masculinity?
I think this is one of the big questions of our time: how can we change that?
Because clearly something went very wrong for young people.”
In the interview, the actor also admitted that he would not enter any prequels or sequels to the Throne War when the series ends next summer, stating that, for him, "battlefields and horses are over."
Starting in November, Kit will take part in a re-edition of Sam Shepard's True West, which is the story of a film producer and his brother, who is a thief.
The actor recently revealed that he considers that finding his wife Rose was the best thing that came out of the Game of Thrones.
“I met my wife in this series, so she somehow gave me my future family and my life from now on,” she said.
Rose played the role of Ygritte, the love interest of Kit's character Jon Snow in the fantasy series awarded Emmys.
The couple knoted in June 2018 on the grounds of Leslie's family estate in Scotland.
HIV/AIDS: China reports a 14% increase in new cases
China has announced a 14% jump in the number of its citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
More than 820 000 people are affected in the country, the health authorities said.
About 40 000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018 alone.
The vast majority of new cases result from transmissions through sexual intercourse, which marks a change from the past.
Traditionally, HIV has spread rapidly in some parts of China as a result of infected blood transfusions.
However, the number of people who contracted HIV in this way has been reduced to virtually zero, Chinese health authorities said at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, in terms of year-on-year variation, the number of those living with HIV and AIDS in China has increased by 100 000 people.
The transmission of HIV through sexual relations is an acute problem in China’s LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against the LGBT community is said to be frequent.
Studies estimated that, due to the country's conservative values, 70 to 90 percent of men who have had sex with other men end up marrying women.
Many of the transmissions of the diseases derive from inappropriate sexual protections in these relationships.
The Chinese government has pledged since 2003 universal access to HIV medication as part of an effort to address the problem.
Maxine Waters denies leaking information about Republican senators by his collaborator and attacks the "dangerous lies" and "conspiracy theories"
U.S. Congresswoman Maxine Waters on Saturday dismissed allegations that a member of her team reportedly published personal information from three U.S. Senate members on lawmakers' Wikipedia pages.
The Los Angeles Democrat declared that the accusations were being promoted by opinion leaders and "ultra-right" websites.
"Lies, lies and more despicable lies," Waters said in a statement on Twitter.
The published information allegedly includes the addresses of the domiciles and telephone numbers of Senators Lindsey Graham of South Carolina and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both from Utah.
The information appeared online on Thursday, published by an unknown person on Capitol Hill during a hearing with a Senate panel on charges of improper sexual conduct against Brett Kavanaugh, appointed to the Supreme Court.
The leak occurred some time after the three senators questioned Kavanaugh.
Conservative websites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState reported that the IP address that identifies the source of the publications was associated with the Waters office and published the data of a Waters staff member, The Hill reported.
"This baseless accusation is totally false and an absolute lie," Waters continued.
"My collaborator, whose identity, personal data and security were compromised by virtue of these fraudulent and false allegations, had no responsibility in this information leak.
This baseless accusation is totally false and an absolute lie.”
Waters' statement quickly drew criticism online, including those of former White House press secretary Ari Fleischer.
"This denial is choleric," Fleischer wrote.
"This suggests that she doesn't have the temperament to be a Congresswoman.
When someone is accused of something that's none of their responsibility, they can't get choleric.
You can’t have a challenging posture.
You can't question the accuser's motives.
You have to reveal calm and serenity.”
Fleischer appeared to compare Waters' reaction to the Democrats' criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, who had been accused by critics of appearing to be too choleric during Thursday's hearing.
Omar Navarro, the Republican nominee who will contest Waters' place in the midterm elections, has also expressed his opinion on Twitter.
"Very serious if it's true," read in his tweet.
In his statement, Waters stated that his office had alerted "the competent authorities" to these fraudulent allegations.
"We ensure that the culprits will be revealed," he continued, "and will be legally held responsible for all of his acts, which are destructive and dangerous to any and all members of my staff."
Criticism of Johnny English Returns to Attacking - Poor parody of espionage film with Rowan Atkinson
It's now fashionable to look for connotations with Brexit in any new film with a British flavor, and such seems in fact to apply itself to the new edition in the series of comedy and action movies Johnny English, started in 2003 with "Johnny English" and brought back to life in 2011 with "The Return of Johnny English."
Is the ironic self-satire on the subject of being so bad the new opportunity for export to the nation?
In any case, the incompetent Johnny English, with his bulging eyes and an excessively elastic expression obtained his license to create confusion, renewed a second time - with his name signaling, more than anything, that it is a comprehensive comical creation aimed at non-Anglophone cinephiles.
He's, of course, the idiot secret agent who, despite his bizarre pretensions to gentle glamour, has a bit of Cloiseau, a hint of Mr. Bean and something of that guy who contributed a single note to the theme of the film "Moments of Glory" at the opening ceremony of the 2012 London Olympics.
In addition, his character is originally based on the traveler and international mystery man whose Atkinson role played in the TV ads for Barclaycard, who left chaos in his wake.
There are one or two interesting moments in this latest edition of JE.
I loved the scene where Johnny English approaches a helicopter wearing medieval armor, with the rotor's shovels briefly making a carillon noise against his helmet.
Atkinson's talent to comedy is evident, but humor sounds weak and strangely superfluous, especially given that "serious" film editions such as 007 and Mission Impossible now confidently propose comedy as an ingredient.
Let’s stick with the idea that humour is directed at kids and not adults and, in my opinion, Johnny English’s bizarre misadventures aren’t as inventive and focused as Atkinson’s mute film numbers in Bean’s character.
Now, the premise, always current, is that Britain is involved in serious trouble.
A computer pirate infiltrated the British super-secret spies network, revealing the identities of all British agents on the ground for consternation of the service agent, a regrettably small role for Kevin Eldon.
It's the last straw for a prime minister who embodies a pompous and conflicting figure, already suffering a total descalation of political unpopularity: Emma Thompson gives her best with this role that reminds the character of Theresa May, but the argument doesn't give much material for great flights.
Her intelligence advisers inform her that since all agents on the asset have become compromised, she will have to get someone out of retirement.
And this someone is the clumsy Johnny English, now working as a teacher in a fancy school, but giving unofficial lessons on how to be an undercover spy: there are some interesting numbers here, because English offers us a spy academy in the style of the movie "Rock School".
English is quickly taken to Whitehall to receive emergency instructions and rejoine with his suffering auxiliary Bough, role played again by Ben Miller.
Bough is now a married man, pegged to a submarine commander, a scruffing character in which Vicki Pepperdine is a little wasted.
Thus Batman and the Robin of the catastrophe in His Majesty's Secret Service are back in action, facing the beautiful fatal woman Ophelia Bulletova, role played by Olga Kurylenko.
Meanwhile, the Prime Minister is being dangerously bewitched by the charismatic technology billionaire who claims to be able to solve Britain's computer problems: the sinister Jason Volta, played by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough begin their odyssey of absurd clumsy acts: disguised as waiters, they set fire to a showy French restaurant, create the confusion penetrating like clandestines in the luxurious yacht of Volta, and English triggers pure anarchy as it tries to use virtual reality glasses to familiarize itself with the interior of Volta’s house.
All the stops were certainly created in view of this last sequence, but as sympathetic and stormy as it is, the set has enough TV for children.
The result is quite average.
And just like Johnny English's other films, I couldn't help but inquire: Can't the British film industry give Rowan Atkinson a role that does justice to his talent?
Labors deny that they are engendering a plan for Britons to work four days a week, but receiving five
Jeremy Corbyn's Labour Party may be weighing a radical plan to provide the British with a four-day working week, but with five days paid.
Reports suggest that the party wants bosses to pass on savings with the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution to workers, granting them a day off supplementary.
Workers would get a three-day weekend, but keeping the orderly.
Sources say that the idea "would be adjusted" to the party's economic programme, which reorients the country in favour of workers.
The passage to a four-day working week obtained the assent of the Congress of Trade Unions as a way for workers to take advantage of changes in the economy.
A senior Labour official told The Sunday Times: "We hope to announce a review of policies before the end of the year.
“It won’t happen overnight, but a four-day working week is an aspiration adjusted to the party’s approach to rebalancing the economy in favour of the worker, as well as the party’s overall strategy for the industrial sector.”
Labour would not be the first to support such an idea, since the Green Party had already proposed a four-day working week during its campaign for the 2017 legislative elections.
However, this aspiration is not favoured at this moment by the entire Labour Party.
A party spokesman said: "The four-day working week is not part of the party's policy and we are not considering it."
Shade finance minister John McDonnell used the party conference last week to reveal his vision of a socialist revolution of the economy.
McDonnell stated that he was determined to regain power from the hands of "faceless directors" and "speculators" of basic service companies.
Its plans also include the non-recovery of the entire share of the current shareholders of water companies, given that the Labor Party will be able to make "deductions" on the basis of the perception of illicit conduct.
McDonnell has also confirmed plans to place workers on the company's boards of directors and set up Inclusive ownership funds that transfer 10% of the share of private companies to employees, who will be able to annually pocket dividends that can amount to 500 pounds.
Lindsey Graham and John Kennedy tell "60 Minutes" if the FBI's investigation into Kavanaugh can make them change their minds
The FBI's investigation into charges against Judge Brett Kavanaugh delayed the final vote of his nomination to the Supreme Court at least for a week, prompting questions about whether the findings could lead Republican senators to withdraw their support.
In an interview broadcast on Sunday, 60-Minute correspondent Scott Pelley asked Republican Senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham if the FBI could discover something that will change their minds.
Kennedy has shown himself but open than his South Carolina colleague.
“I mean, of course,” Kennedy said.
"I said when I was going to the hearing, I said, I spoke to Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him after this happened when this allegation appeared, and I asked him, "Did you do it"?
He was resolute, determined, unequivocal.”
Graham's vote, however, seems unmovable.
"I am decided on Brett Kavanaugh and an explosive charge would be needed," he said.
"Dr. Ford, I don't know what happened, but I do know one thing: Brett effected a vigorous denial," Graham added, referring to Christine Blasey Ford.
"And everyone she mentioned failed to confirm it.
It was 36 years ago.
“I don’t see anything new to change.”
What is the Global Citizen Festival? Has you done anything to reduce poverty?
This Saturday, New York City will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual musical event with a list of extremely impressive stars who will perform on an equally impressive mission: to eradicate poverty in the world.
Already in its seventh year, the Global Citizen Festival will host tens of thousands of people in the Grand Lawn of Central Park, not only to attend performances such as those of Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to reinforce the awareness of the true goal of the event: to eradicate extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, started in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an advocacy group that hopes to put an end to poverty by increasing the number of people who actively combat it.
In order to receive a free ticket for the event (unless they are willing to pay for a VIP ticket) viewers had to perform a series of tasks, or "actions", such as volunteering, sending an email to a world leader, a phone call or any other significant ways that contribute to increasing awareness for the goal of putting an end to poverty.
However, what has been Global Citizen’s degree of success in these 12 years in achieving its goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free concert a genuine way of persuading them to demand action, or is this just another case of "cliquetivism" - do we think it makes it a difference to sign a petition online or send a tweet?
Global Citizen says that since 2011 there have been more than 19 million “actions” of its supporters who have put pressure to achieve a number of different objectives.
These actions, according to the organization, contributed to world leaders having announced commitments and policies valued at more than 37 billion dollars, which will affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group cited 390 commitments and announcements arising from its shares, with at least 10 billion dollars referring to them having already been disbursed or raised.
The group estimates that the funds obtained have so far had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people around the world.
Some of the key commitments include The Power of Nutrition, a UK-based partnership bringing together investors and implementers committed to "helping children reach their full potential," which promised to give Rwanda 35 million dollars to help end malnutrition in the country. This after receiving more than 4700 tweets from Global Citizens.
“With the support of the UK government, donors, national governments and global citizens like all of us, we can make the social injustice of malnutrition a footnote to history,” Tracey Ullman, an ambassador for The Power of Nutrition, told a crowd at a live concert in London in April 2018.
The group further stated that, after more than 5000 actions of pressure on the United Kingdom to improve the nutrition of mothers and children, the government announced that it would finance a project, Power of Nutrition, which will cover 5 million women and children, with interventions at the level of nutrition.
In response to one of the site's frequently asked questions, "What makes you think we can eradicate extreme poverty?"
Global Citizen responds: "It's been a long and tortuous path - sometimes we fall and fail.
But just like the great civil and anti-apartheid rights movements that preceded us because we are more powerful together.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B and Janelle Monáe are among the artists who will perform at this year’s event in New York, hosted by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
U.S U.S. may use navy as "blocking" to put an obstacle to Russia's energy exports - Interior Secretary
"If necessary," Washington can turn to its navy to prevent Russian energy from reaching markets, including in the Middle East, U.S. Interior Secretary Ryan Zinke said.
Zinke claimed that Russia's involvement in Syria, in particular, which intervenes at the invitation of the legitimate government, is a pretext to explore new energy markets.
"I believe that the reason they are in the Middle East is that they want to intermediate the energy, as they do in Eastern Europe, in the belly south of Europe," he said.
And according to the ruler, there are ways and means to prevent it.
"The United States has the ability, with its navy, to ensure that seaways are open and, if necessary, to carry out blockades to ensure that energy does not reach the market," he said.
Zinke was addressed to participants at an event organized by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-profit group calling itself "the voice of the energy consumer" in the United States.
In his speech, he went on comparing Washington’s approaches to relationships with Russia and Iran, stating that they are effectively the same.
“The economic option in Iran and Russia is more or less to leverage and replace fuels,” he said, while referring to Russia as a “magician of a single trick” with an economy dependent on fossil fuels.
These statements come at a time when the Trump administration finds itself on a mission to increase exports of natural gas to Europe, replacing Russia, which is the far cheapest option for European consumers.
To this end, Trump administration officials, including the president himself, are trying to persuade Germany to abandon the “inadequate” Nord Stream pipeline project, which according to Trump made Berlin a “prisoner” from Moscow.
Moscow has repeatedly stated that the Nord Stream pipeline, budgeted at US$11 billion, aims to double the existing capacity to 110 billion cubic meters, is a purely economic project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington’s fervent opposition to the project is motivated simply by economic motives, with an example of unfair competition.
"I believe that we share the view that energy cannot be an instrument to exert pressure and that consumers should have the ability to choose suppliers," Russian Energy Minister Aleksandr Novak said following a meeting with U.S. Energy Secretary Rick Perry in Moscow in September.
The U.S. position prompted a negative reaction from Germany, which reaffirmed its commitment to the project.
Germany's main industrial association, the Federation of German Industries (BDI), has called on the US to stay away from EU energy policy and bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
“I have a big problem when a third state interferes with our energy supply,” said Dieter Kempf, head of the Federation of German Industries (BDI) in the wake of a recent meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elizabeth Warren “deeplishly analyzes” the possibility of running for president in 2020, the Massachusetts Senator said
Massachusetts Senator Elizabeth Warren said on Saturday she would "deeply analyze" the possibility of running for president after the midterm elections.
During an informal public debate in Holyoke, Massachusetts, Warren confirmed that he would ponder the possibility of applying.
“It’s time for women to go to Washington to repair our dysfunctional government, and this includes a woman as a top official,” he said.
“After November 6, I’ll look deeply at the possibility of running for president.”
Warren commented on President Donald Trump during the informal debate, noting that he "was leading the country in the wrong direction."
"I'm extremely concerned about what Donald Trump is doing to our democracy," he said.
Warren has been a harsh vocal critique of Trump and his nominee for the Supreme Court, Brett Kavanaugh.
In a tweet on Friday, Warren said: "It's clear that we need an FBI investigation before the vote."
However, a poll conducted on Thursday, revealed that a majority of Warren's own constituents are not of the opinion that she should run for president in 2020.
59% of Massachusetts “likely” voters said the senator should not run, according to the Suffolk/Boston Globe University’s Political Research Center probe.
32% support your candidacy.
The poll revealed superior support for a candidacy of former Governor Governor Deval Patrick, with 38% in favor of a potential candidacy and 48% against it.
Other prominent Democratic Party names debated about a potential candidacy in 2020 include former Vice President Joe Biden and Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders.
Biden claimed he would officially decide in January, according to the Associated Press.
Sarah Palin mentions the post-traumatic stress of Track Palin at Donald Trump rally
Track Palin, aged 26, spent a year in Iraq following his enlistment in September.
On Monday night, he was detained and charged with an incident of domestic violence
"What my own son is going through, what's going on after I've returned, I can identify with the other families who feel the ramifications of post-traumatic stress and some of the wounds with which our soldiers, in fact, come back," he told the audience at a rally in support of Donald Trump in Tulsa, Oklahoma.
Palin called this detention "the elephant in the room" and stated that his son and other veterans "come back a little different, come back hardened, come back inquiring if there is this respect for what his comrade soldiers and aviators, and all the other members of the armed forces, gave to the country."
Track Palin was detained Monday in Wasilla, Alaska, and charged with domestic violence over a woman and gun possession while drunk, according to Dan Bennett, a spokeswoman for the Wasilla Police Department.
Eighteen states and the District of Columbia support the challenge of the new asylum policy
Eighteen states and the District of Columbia are giving their support to a legal challenge to a new U.S. policy that refuses asylum to victims fleeing gang or domestic violence.
Representatives from the 18 states and the district submitted an amicus curiae (court friends) statement on Friday in Washington, in support of an asylum seeker who is contesting that policy, NBC News reported.
The complainant's full name in the Grace vs. Sessions lawsuit that the American Civil Liberties Union filed in August against the policy was not revealed.
The person stated that her partner "and her children, members of a violent gang," abused her, but U.S. authorities rejected her asylum application on July 20.
The plaintiff was arrested in Texas.
The public ministries that support Grace described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, which produce a large number of asylum seekers in the U.S., such as nations facing widespread problems with gangs and domestic violence.
The new U.S. asylum policy repealed a 2014 decision by the Immigrant Resources Board that allowed undocumented immigrants evaded domestic violence to apply for asylum.
District Attorney-General Karl Racine said in a statement on Friday that the new policy “ignores decades of state, federal and international laws.”
"Federal legislation requires that all asylum applications be assessed on the basis of the specific facts and circumstances underlying them; this interdiction violates that principle," reads the amicus curiae statement.
Lawyers also argued in this statement that police interdiction of immigrant entry negatively affects the U.S. economy, noting that they are more likely to become entrepreneurs and "make available the necessary manpower."
Attorney General Jeff Sessions has ordered immigration judges to stop granting asylum to victims fleeing domestic violence and gangs in June.
"Asylum is available to those leaving their country due to persecution or fears based on ethnicity, religion, nationality or belonging to a specific social group or with a certain political opinion," Sessions said in his announcement on June 11.
Asylum has never been intended to alleviate all problems, not even all the serious problems, that people face every day around the world.
Desperate rescue efforts in Palu when the death toll doubles in a clock to find survivors
For survivors, the situation is increasingly desperate.
"The environment is very tense," said Risa Kusuma, 35, the mother of a febrile male baby, while comforting him at an evacuation center in the devastated city of Palu.
“Every minute, an ambulance brings in more bodies.
There's a shortage of clean water."
Residents who returned to their destroyed homes were sighted to try to save everything they could find among their water-stewed belongings.
There were hundreds injured and the hospitals, damaged by the 7.5-magnitude quake, were overwhelmed.
Some of the wounded, such as Dwi Haris, who fractured his ribs and shoulder, rested outside Palu's Army Hospital, where patients were treated abroad due to the strong aftershocks that continued.
His eyes were bathed in tears when he reported what he felt when the violent earthquake shook him in the fifth-floor hotel room where he was with his wife and daughter.
“There was no time to save ourselves.
I was trapped in the ruins of the wall, I believe," Haris told the Associated Press, adding that his family was in town for a wedding.
"I heard my wife scream for help, but then silence.
I don't know what happened to her and my daughter.
I hope you're safe."
The U.S. ambassador accuses China of "intimidation" with "propaganda ads"
A week after an official Chinese newspaper published a four-page announcement in a U.S. daily newspaper promoting the mutual benefits of trade between the United States and China, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using the U.S. press to spread propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump referred last Wednesday to China Daily's paid supplement at the Des Moines Register, China's most-drawn newspaper after accusing China of seeking to interfere in the November 6 congressional election, an indictment denied by China.
Trump’s accusation that Beijing was trying to interfere in the U.S
While it is normal for foreign governments to publish announcements to promote trade, Beijing and Washington are currently waging an ever-increasing trade war that has seen both sides apply waves of tariffs on imports from each other.
China’s retaliatory tariffs in the early stages of the trade war are designed to affect exporters in states that support Trump’s Republican Party, Chinese and U.S. experts claim.
Terry Branstad, the U.S. ambassador to China and a former long-time governor of Iowa, a major exporter of agricultural products to China, claimed Beijing has harmed American workers, farmers and businesses.
China, Branstad wrote in an opinion piece in Sunday's Des Moines Register, "is now setting foot on the accelerator of this intimidation with the publication of propaganda advertisements in our free press."
"With the spread of this propaganda, the Chinese government is taking advantage of the cherished American tradition of freedom of expression and the press by publishing a paid announcement in the Des Moines Register," Branstad said.
"On the other hand, in the stalls here in Beijing, we find very few dissenting voices and we will never see a real reflection of the disparity of opinions that the Chinese may have of their country's worrying economic trajectory, as the media are under the firm control of the Chinese Communist Party," he wrote.
In addition, he added that "one of the most prominent Chinese newspapers refused the offer to publish" its article, although it did not mention which.
Republicans alienate female voters ahead of mid-term elections with Kavanauh disaster, analysts warn
At a time when many leading Republicans support and defend Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh in the face of the various accusations of sexual assault, analysts warn that this could provoke a negative reaction, particularly from women, during the midterm elections to take place soon.
The emotions surrounding the case have been very strong and a majority of Republicans have already publicly stated that they intend to move towards a vote.
These things don't go backwards," he told The Hill Grant Reeher, a professor of political science at Syracuse University's Maxwell School, for an article published Saturday.
Reeher said he doubts Senator Jeff Flake’s last-minute pressure to conduct an FBI investigation is enough to appease angry voters.
“Women won’t forget what happened yesterday – they won’t forget tomorrow or November,” said Karene Jean-Pierre, a senior adviser and national spokesman for the progressive group MoveOn, according to the Washington newspaper, D.C.
On Friday morning, Protestants sang "November is at the door!" when they demonstrated in the Senate atrium, given that Republicans controlling the Judiciary Committee chose to move ahead with Kavanaugh's nomination despite the testimony of Dr. Christine Blasey Ford, the Mic reported.
"The Democrats' enthusiasm and motivation are going to beat all records," Stu Rothenberg, a political analyst without party affiliation, told the website.
“People say it’s already high; it’s true.
But it may be even higher, particularly among indecisive voters in suburban areas and younger voters, aged 18 to 29, who, while they don’t like the president, often don’t vote.”
Even before Ford's public testimony describing his allegations of sexual assault against the nominee for the Supreme Court, analysts suggested that a negative reaction could occur if Republicans moved forward with confirmation.
“This has become a mess for the Republican Party,” said Michael Steele, a former president of the National Republican Committee early last week, according to NBC News.
"It's not just about the committee's vote, or whether Kavanaugh is going to take his place as a judge. It's also about the way Republicans have handled the matter and how they've treated it," Guy Cecil, director of Priorities USA, a group that helps elect Democrats, told the news channel.
However, Americans appear to be somewhat divided about who to believe after Ford and Kavanaugh's testimonies, with a small majority aligning with the former.
A new YouGov poll revealed that 41 percent of respondents believed absolutely or likely in Ford’s testimony, while 35 percent stated that they believed absolutely or probably Kavanaugh.
In addition, 38 percent stated that they considered that Kavanaugh had likely or absolutely lied in his testimony, while only 30 percent stated the same about Ford.
Following Flake's proposal, the FBI initiated an investigation of Ford's claims, as well as from another accuser, Deborah Ramirez, according to the Guardian.
Ford said under oath before the Senate Judicial Committee last week that Kavanaugh had sexually molested her while alcoholic when she was 17.
Ramirez contends that the nominee for the Supreme Court exposed his genitals when the two attended a party during the period they both studied at Yale in the 1980s.
The inventor of the World Wide Web intends to create a new Internet to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, inventor of the World Wide Web, created a startup that aims to rival Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest project of this legendary character of technologies, Inrupt, is a company that takes advantage of Berners-Lee's open-spoke source platform, Solid.
This allows users to decide where their data is stored and who has access to what information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee jokingly stated that Inrupt’s underlying intention is to “dominate the world.”
“We have to do it now,” she said.
“It’s an historical moment.”
The app utilizes Solid's technology to enable people to create their own "online personal data warehouse," or POD.
This may contain contact lists, to-do lists, calendar, music library and other personal and professional tools.
It's as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available in one browser and simultaneously.
What is unique about the personal warehouse of online data is that it is exclusively up to the user to decide on who has access to what kind of information.
The company calls it "personal empowerment through the data."
Inrupt’s idea, according to John Bruce’s CEO, is to provide adequate resources, processes and skills to help make Solid available to everyone.
The company is currently comprised of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IMB, some internal programmers hired to work on the project, and a community of programmers on a volunteer basis.
Starting this week, technology creators around the world will be able to create their own decentralized applications using the tools available on Inrupt’s website.
Berners-Lee stated that he and his team aren't talking to "Facebook and Google about whether or not to introduce a total change in which all their business models are turned upside overnight.
"We're not asking for permission."
In a publication on Medium on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt’s “mission is to create commercial energy and an ecosystem that helps protect the integrity and quality of the new Web created with Solid.”
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he created the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the debate over Internet neutrality.
Even as he launches Inrupt, Berners-Lee will continue to be founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm incredibly optimistic about this next era of the Web," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: homage to cleric decorated with Victoria Cross in the First World War
The only cleric of the Church of England to receive the Victoria Cross during the First World War as a combator was honored in his hometown after 100 years.
Lieutenant Colonel Reverend Bernard Vann obtained the decoration on September 29, 19118 with the attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
However, he was killed by a sniper four days later and never knew he received the highest British military honour.
His two grandchildren unveiled a commemorative plaque at a stop in Rushden, Northamptonshire, on Saturday.
One of them, Michael Vann, claimed it was "brilliantly symbolic" that the plaque had been presented exactly 100 years after the feat he gave the award to his grandfather.
According to the London Gazette on September 29, 1911, Lieutenant Colonel Vann led his battalion on the crossing of the Saint-Quentin Channel "in a very thick fog and under closed fire of portable guns and machine guns".
Then ran to the line of fire and, "with the greatest courage," led the line forward before single-handedly attacking a cannon and slaughtering three soldiers of the detachment.
Lieutenant Colonel Vann was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just over a month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann, 72, said his grandfather's actions were "something I know I could never match, but also a lesson in humility."
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, laid a wreath after the parade, led by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann stated that he "felt very honored to participate in the parade" and added that "the courage of a genuine hero is being demonstrated through the support that will be given by many people."
Fans of mixed martial arts woke up all night to see Bellator 206 watch instead Peppa Pig
Imagine this: you stay up all night to see Bellator 206, full of vedetas and, in the end, is prevented from seeing the main event.
The San José series included 13 matches, with six from the main group, and was airing live overnight in the UK on Channel 5.
At six in the morning, when Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing for the clash, viewers in the UK were amazed when coverage switched to Peppa Nut.
Some didn't impress themselves after being awake until dawn especially for combat.
One fan on Twitter described the move to children's cartoons as "a kind of bad-tasting joke."
"Government regulation says the content is not suitable for six in the morning, so they had to switch to other programming," Dave Schwartz, administrator with marketing and communications, said when asked about the broadcast.
""Peppa Pig," yes."
Bellator President Scott Coker said it would rectify the timing of broadcasts to include UK spectators in the future.
“I believe that when I think about repetition, I think we can solve the problem,” Coker said.
"But it's sies Sunday morning there and we won't be able to solve it until our Sunday hour, which it's Monday at British time.
But we're working on it.
I'm not lying when I say that when the programming changed, there were a lot of messages exchanged and none very nice.
We thought it was a technical mistake.
But it wasn't: it was the government's problem.
I promise it won't happen next time.
We'll reduce the number of fights to five instead of six, as we usually do. We've tried to exceed fans' expectations and exaggerated.
It's an unfortunate situation."
Derester Island Discs: Tom Daley Felt "inferior" as to His sexuality
Olympic diver Tom Daley said he had grown to feel inferior to everyone due to his sexuality, but that gave him motivation to become a success.
The 24-year-old said he didn't realize until he entered high school that "not everyone is like me."
In an interview with Radion 4’s first Desert Island Discs presented by Lauren Laverne, she said she has spoken out in favor of gay rights to give “hope” to others.
He also stated that becoming a father made him worry less about winning the Olympics.
The show's long-standing regular presenter, Kirsty Young, took a break from several months by virtue of illness.
Appearing as a pariah on Laverne’s first show, Daley said he felt “lower” than everyone else because “it wasn’t socially acceptable to like boys and girls.”
“To this day, this impression of feeling smaller and different has been the real motive that has given me the power and strength to achieve success.”
His goal was to prove that he was "someone," he said, so as not to disappoint everyone when they discovered their sexuality sooner or later.
The double Olympic bronze medalist has become a prominent LGBT activist and used his participation in this year’s Commonwealth Games in Australia to appeal to more countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
According to him, he manifested himself because he was happy to be able to live openly without consequences and intended to give "hope" to others.
The three-time world champion said that falling in love with a man, American filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013, "caught me by surprise."
Daley married last year the Oscar winner, 20 years older, although he claimed that the age gap was never a problem.
"When we went through so much when we're so young" - his first appearance at the Olympics was at the age of 14 and his father died of cancer three years later - he said, it's hard to find someone of the same age who went through similar ups and downs.
The couple had a son last year in June, named Robert Ray Black-Daley, and Daley claimed "his entire perspective" had changed.
"If I had been asked last year, all that mattered was "winning a gold medal,"" he said.
"But you know? There are things bigger than Olympic gold medals.
"My Olympic gold medal is Robbie."
Your son has the same name as his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after being diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said his father didn't accept the fact that he's going to die and one of the last things he asked was if they already had tickets to the London games in 2012, because he wanted to be in the front row.
"I couldn't tell you that "you won't be around so you're going to be in the front row, Dad,"" he said.
"I was holding him no hand when he stopped breathing and that's only when he really stopped breathing and died that I finally recognized that he wasn't invincible," he said.
The following year, Daley competed at the 2012 Olympics and won a bronze medal.
“I was fully aware that it was what I’d dreamed of my whole life: diving in front of a crowd of compatriots at the Olympic Games ... there was no better feeling.”
All this was also the inspiration of her first choice of song - Proud, by Heather Small - with which she had identified herself during the preparation for the Olympics, and which still gives her chicken skin.
Desert Island Discs runs on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11.5pm British summer time.
Mickelson bass on the bench at the Ryder Cup on Saturday
American Phil Mickelson will hit a record on Saturday when he plays his 47th match in a Ryder Cup, but will have to regain his form to prevent the milestone from symbolising an unfortunate occasion.
Mickelson, playing in the biennial event for the 12th time, a attendance record, was sent to the bench by captain Jim Furyk in Saturday's fourballs and foursomes.
Instead of being at the center of the action, as it has so often been in U.S. teams, the five-time winner of the men’s main championships has divided the day between the role of supporter and the work in his game on the field, hoping to rectify what afflicts him.
Never having taken strokes of the straightest even at the peak of his career, the 48-year-old is not ideal for the National Le Golf course, where the long rough normally punishes the wandering strokes.
And if the pitch alone wasn't intimidating enough, Mickelson, in Sunday's ninth match, will face the precise British Open champion Francesco Molinari, who has teamed with beginner Tommy Fleetwood, winning all four matches played by them this week.
If the Americans, with a four-point disadvantage starting the 12 single matches, register a good start, Mickelson's match could prove absolutely crucial.
Furyk expressed confidence in his man, but he couldn't say much more.
"He totally understood the role he had today, patted me on the back, put his arm around me and said he'd be ready tomorrow," Furyk said.
"He has a lot of confidence in himself.
He's a respected champion who've given these teams a lot in the past, and also this week.
I probably hadn't stared to see him playing two matches.
I've thought about more, but that's how it happened and that's how we thought we'd have to proceed.
He wants to be in the game, just like everyone else.”
Mickelson will on Sunday surpass Nick Faldo's record for the highest number of matches played in the Ryder Cup.
This could mark the end of a Ryder Cup career that has never matched the successes of your individual resume.
Mickelson has 18 wins, 20 losses and seven draws, although Furyk has claimed that his presence has created some intangible advantages for the team.
"He's sarcastic, funny and witty, he likes to play with people and he's an excellent person to have in the team room," he explained.
"I think the younger players had fun messing with him too this week, which was fun to see.
He's much more than just playing."
European captain Thomas Bjorn knows that his great advantage could disappear in an apex
Thomas Bjorn, European captain, knows from experience that his slack lead upon entering the last day of singles in the Ryder Cup can easily become a bumpy ride.
The Danes had their debut in the 1997 match in Valderrama, where a team captained by Seve Ballesteros maintained a five-point advantage over the Americans, but crossed the goal line with their nose with the narrowest of margins, winning 141⁄2-131⁄2.
"We always tried to remember that we had a considerable advantage in Valderrama; we had a great advantage in Brookline, where we lost, and in Valderrama, where we won, but narrowly," said Bjorn, in the image, after watching the 2018 5-3 Class victory, both on Friday and yesterday, to lead 10-5 at Le Golf National.
Thus, history has shown me and all who are in this team that this is not over yet.
Tomorrow, do your best.
Go out there and do everything right.
This doesn't end until we have the points on the marker.
We have a goal that is to try to win this trophy and that's where the focus should remain.
“I’ve always said I’ve focused on the 12 players on our side, but we’re well aware of who’s on the other side: the best players in the world.”
Enchanted by the performance of his players on a difficult golf course, Bjorn added: "I would never get ahead of myself in that.
It's a different challenge tomorrow.
Tomorrow what matters is the individual performances and this involves a different task.
It's great that we're there with a partner when things go well, but when we're there on our own, our skills as golfers are tested to the maximum.
This is the message you need to convey to the players: tomorrow, do your best.
Now they're going to abandon their partner and he'll have to go and give the best of himself too."
In contrast to Bjorn, opponent Jim Furyk will look for his players to perform better individually than as partners, constituting the exceptions Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who have won three points out of four.
Furyk himself was already on both sides of these big twists and turns on the last day, having been part of the winning team in Brookline before he ended up losing when Europe took "Miracle in Medinah".
"I remember all the words," he said in response to a question about the way Ben Crenshaw, captain in 1999, motivated his players for the final day.
"We have 12 important matches tomorrow, but you'd like to get that quick start like what you've seen in Brookline and what you saw in Medinah.
When this impetus heads to one side, the pressure is too great in these middle matches.
We've organised our staff accordingly, and we've ordered the players the way we feel, you know we're going to try to do magic tomorrow."
Thomas, with the mission of trying to lead the counterattack, faces Rory McIlroy in the main match, Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter the other Europeans in the first half of the planning.
"I chose this group of players in this order because I think it covers the whole route," Bjorn said of his choices for the singles.
New German warship once again postponed
The latest German navy frigate was supposed to enter service in 2014 to replace Cold Wartime warships. However, it will be necessary to await at least by next year, due to systems prone to malfunctions and an exponentially growing cost, according to local media outlets.
The entry into the service of "Rheinland-Pfalz," the commanding ship of the all-new Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigates, was now postponed until the first half of 2019, according to Die Zeit newspaper that quoted a military spokesman.
The vessel was supposed to join the navy in 2014, but unsettling post-delivery problems undermined the fate of the ambitious project.
The four Baden-Wuerttemberg-class vessels that the navy had ordered in 2007 will serve to replace the aged Bremen-class frigates.
It is known that they will be equipped with a powerful cannon, in addition to a series of anti-aircraft missiles and anti-vessels, as well as some stealth technologies such as the reduction of radar, infrared and acoustic signatures.
Other important features include more dilated periods between maintenance, which would make it possible to send the latest frigates on missions with a maximum duration of two years away from the ports of origin.
However, the continuing delays have caused state-of-the-art warships, regarded as a way for Germany to project power across borders, will already be outdated when they enter service, Die Zeit said.
The unlucky frigate F125 made headlines last year when the German navy refused to put it into service, having returned it to Blohm & Voss shipyards in Hamburg.
This was the first time the navy had returned a ship to the shipyard after delivery.
Little is known about the reasons for the devolution, but the German media cite a series of crucial "hardware and software defects" that would render the warship useless if used in a combat mission.
The shortcomings of the software were particularly important, since Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships will be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors, only half of the contingent required in the oldest Bremen-class frigates.
It was also known that the vessel is excessively heavy, which reduces its performance and limits the navy’s ability to install additional features in the future.
It is believed that the "Rheinland-Pfalz", of 7000 tons, has twice the weight of ships of similar classes used by Germans in World War II.
In addition to hardware failures, the price of the entire project, including crew formation, is also becoming a problem.
It is said that this will have reached an astonishing 3.1 billion euros, an increase compared to the 2.2 billion initially budgeted.
The problems plaguing the latest frigates have become particularly important in the light of recent warnings that German naval power is weakening.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, leader of the German parliamentary defence committee, acknowledged that the navy is currently running out of ships capable of being used.”
The politician stated that the problem grew exponentially over time, as older ships were put out of service without vessels to replace them.
In the end, he lamented that none of the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigates were able to join the navy.
National Trust eavesdrops secret life of bats
New investigations are being carried out on property in the Scottish Highlands with the aim of revealing how bats use the landscape to search for food.
It is expected that the results will shine some light on the behavior of these unique flying mammals and help guide conservation activities in the future.
The study, conducted by scientists from the National Trust for Scotland, will follow common and fruit bats, as well as long-eared browns and Daubenton's bats in the Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special engravers will be placed in key locations scattered around the property to follow the activities of the bats throughout the station.
Movable studies will also be carried out by NTS staff and volunteers with portable detectors.
Analysis by experts of all the recordings will assess the frequency of bat calls and what each species is doing.
A map of the habitat will then be produced to create a detailed picture of their behaviors on a territorial scale.
Rob Dewar, an advisor for nature conservation at NTS, expects the results to reveal which habitat areas are most important for bats and the way they are used by each species.
This information will help determine the benefits of habitat management work, such as meadow breeding, as well as how best to maintain forests for bats and associated species.
Bat populations in Scotland and across the United Kingdom have declined considerably over the last century.
These animals are threatened by the construction and development work that affects the dormitories and causes habitat to be scarce.
Wind turbines and lightnings are also a threat, in conjunction with fly-killing papers and some chemical treatments of building materials, in addition to attacks by domestic cats.
In fact, bats aren’t blind.
However, given their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful than their eyes when it comes to catching prey.
It is used by them a sophisticated echo location technique to identify insects and obstacles in their flight trajectory.
The NTS, responsible for the care of more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76 000 hectares of land throughout the region, takes bats very seriously.
The organization has ten trained specialists who regularly carry out studies, inspections of dormitories and sometimes rescues.
He also created the first reserve in Scotland dedicated exclusively to bats, on the Threave estate in Dumfries and Galloway, which houses eight of the ten Scottish bat species.
The director of the property, David Thompson, says that it is the ideal territory for them.
"Here in Threave we've got an excellent area for bats," he said.
"We've got the old buildings, a lot of veteran trees and all this good habitat.
However, there is still much about the bats that we don’t know about, so the work we’ve done here and the other properties will help us learn more about what they need to flourish.”
It was highlighted by him the importance of verifying if there are bats before performing maintenance on the properties, because the unintentional destruction of only one maternity dormitory can lead to the death of up to 400 females and young animals, which could eradicate the entirety of the local population.
Bats are a protected species and it is illegal to kill them, harass them, disturb them, or destroy their dormitories.
Elisabeth Ferrell, Scottish head of the Bat Conservation Trust, encouraged the public to participate to help.
“We still have a lot to learn about our bats, and for many of our species, we simply don’t know how their populations are avenging.”
Ronaldo belies rape charges as his lawyers prepare to prosecute German newspaper
Cristiano Ronaldo has characterised allegations of rape against him as "false news," stating that people "want to promote themselves" using his name.
His lawyers are preparing to sue the German news magazine Der Spiegel, which published the allegations.
The Portuguese and Juventus national team forward was accused of raping an American woman, identified as Kathryn Mayorga, in a hotel room in Las Vegas in 2009.
It is alleged that it will then have paid 375 000 dollars for the alleged victim to remain silent about the accident, it read on Friday's Der Spiegel.
Speaking on a live video on Instagram to his 142 million followers hours after the release of the allegations, 33-year-old Ronaldo characterized the accounts as "fake news."
"No, no, no, no.
What they said today, fake news," the winner of five Golden Balls told the camera.
“They want to promote themselves at the expense of my name.
That's normal.
They want to be famous saying my name, but that's part of the profession.
I'm a happy man and it's okay," the player added with a smile.
Ronaldo's lawyers are preparing to sue Der Spiegel over the allegations, which they characterized as "an inadmissible account of suspicions in the field of privacy," according to Reuters.
Advocate Christian Schertz stated that the player would demand compensation for moral damages in the amount corresponding to the severity of the offence, which is probably one of the most serious violations of personal rights in recent years.”
The alleged incident took place in June 2009 in a suite of the Palms Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas.
After meeting in a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga allegedly went to the player's room, where the player's room will have raped her anally, according to documents submitted to the Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga alleges that Ronaldo put himself on his knees after the alleged incident and told him that it was "99 percent" "a good guy," harmed by the "one percent."
The documents allege that Ronaldo confirmed that the couple had sex, but that it was consensual.
Mayorga alleges that he addressed the police and had photographs of his injuries taken in a hospital, but that he later agreed to enter into an extrajudicial settlement because he felt "terrified by the prospect of retaliation" and was concerned about the possibility of "being publicly humiliated."
The 34-year-old woman now claims she intends to overturn the deal as she remains traumatized by the alleged incident.
Ronaldo was about to join Real Madrid from Manchester United at the time of the alleged aggression. This summer he moved to the Italian giants Juve in a business of 100 million euros.
Brexix: UK 'would repent" from losing car manufacturers
The UK "would repent forever" if it lost its status as a world leader in the manufacture of automobiles following Brexit, Business Secretary Greg Clark said.
Clark also said that Toyota in the United Kingdom had told the BBC that if Britain left the EU without a deal, it would temporarily stop production from its plant in Burnaston near Derby.
“We need an agreement,” Mr. Clark said.
The Japanese manufacturer said that the impact of border delays in the case of a no-deal Brexit could lead to the loss of jobs.
The Burnaston plant, which produces Toyota's Auris and Avensis models, produced almost 150 000 automobiles last year, 90% of which were exported to the rest of the European Union.
“My perspective is that if Britain leaves the EU disorderly at the end of March, we’ll see production stops at our plant,” said Marvin Cooke, Toyota’s chief executive at Burnaston.
Other car manufacturers in the UK have expressed concerns about leaving the EU without an agreement on the operation of cross-border transactions, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover.
BMW, for example, said it would close its Minis plant in Oxford for a month after Brexit.
The main concerns concern what manufacturers claim are supply chain risks in the event of a no-deal Brexit.
Toyota's production line is managed on a "just-in-time" basis, with parts coming every 37 minutes, coming from suppliers in both the UK and the EU, for custom-made cars.
If the U.K
It would be impossible for Toyota to maintain more than a day of stocks at its Derbyshire plant, the company said, so production would have to stop.
Clark said Theresa May's "Chequers Plan" for future relations with the EU was "calibrated precisely to avoid these border checks."
"We have to have an agreement. We want to have the best deal that allows, as I said, not only the current success, but also the use by us of this opportunity," he told the BBC's Radio 4's Today programme.
"The evidence not only from Toyota but also from other manufacturers points out that we absolutely need to be able to proceed with what has been a highly successful set of supply chains."
Although it could not give an estimate of stopping time, Toyota said that in the long term, increased costs would reduce the plant’s competitiveness and could sooner or later lead to the loss of jobs.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who worked at the Burnaston plant for 24 years and coordinates the Unite syndicate at that site, stated that its members are increasingly concerned: "According to my experience, if they are lost, these jobs will never return.
A government spokesman stated that "We presented an accurate and credible plan for our future relationship with the EU."
Trump meeting with Rosenstein could be postponed again, White House says
Donald Trump’s important meeting with Assistant Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could be “delayed another week” since there has yet to be an understanding of Brett Kavanaugh, appointed to the Supreme Court, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of legal adviser Robert Mueller, who is investigating Russian interference in elections, links between Trump's aides and Russia, and possible obstruction of justice by the president.
The fact that Trump may be preparing the dismissal of the deputy attorney general, thus endangering Mueller's independence, has been fueling rumors in Washington for months.
Earlier this month the New York Times reported that Rosenstein discussed the possibility of disingenuously recording talks with Trump and the possibility of deposing the President through the 25th Amendment.
Rosenstein denied the information.
But last Monday he went to the White House, there being information that he was about to resign.
Instead, a meeting was announced on Thursday with Trump, who was at the United Nations in New York.
Trump said he "would prefer not to fire" Rosenstein, but the meeting was then postponed to avoid overwhesition with a Senate Justice Committee hearing in which they witnessed Kavanaugh and one of the women who accused him of sexual harassment, Dr. Christine Blasey Ford.
On Friday, Trump ordered a one-week FBI investigation into the charges against Kavanaugh, delaying the final vote in the Senate.
Trump's press officer, Sarah Sanders, took part in Fox News Sunday.
Asked about the meeting with Rosenstein, he said: "The date has not yet been marked, it may be this week but it can also be postponed another week, in view of everything that has happened in the Supreme Court.
Let's see. I always like to keep the press informed."
Some journalists wouldn't agree with such a claim: Sanders hasn't organised a White House press conference since Sept. 10.
Presenter Chris Wallace asked why.
Sanders said the shortage of press conferences was not due to an aversion to the "protagonism" of television reporters, although he said: "I wouldn't disagree with the fact that they like the protagonism."
It then implied that direct contact between Trump and the press will increase.
"The President does more Q&A sessions than any previous president," he said, adding, without gathering evidence: "We've analyzed those figures."
The press conferences will continue to take place, Sanders said, but "if the press has the opportunity to ask questions directly to the President of the United States, that's infinitely better than talking to me.
We've tried to do that often, and they've seen that we've done it over the last few weeks. The fact that they can speak to the President of the United States replaces any press conference."
Trump regularly answers questions when leaving the White House or participating in open sessions or press conferences, upon receiving foreign representatives.
Solo press conferences are rare.
This week the President showed why in New York, appearing in a relaxed and sometimes bizarre way before the journalists present.
Health minister writes to EU workers at the Scottish National Health Service over Brexit fears
The Health Minister has written to EU staff working at the Scottish National Health Service to express the country's gratitude and request that they remain after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman, MSP, sent the letter less than six months away from the UK's departure from the EU.
The Scottish Government has already pledged to fund applications for the resident status of EU citizens working in the country's decentralised public services.
In her letter, Mrs Freeman wrote: "Throughout the summer continued negotiations between the UK and the EU on withdrawal, with decisions being expected for this autumn.
But the UK government has also accelerated preparations for a possible no-deal exit scenario.
I know this is a very unsettling time for all of you.
That’s why I’d like to reiterate at the moment how much I value the contribution of each employee, regardless of their nationalities.
Peers from all over the EU and from other backgrounds bring valuable experience and skills, which strengthen and improve the work of the health service by benefiting the patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is truly your home and we very much want you to stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie undergoing emergency surgery after suffering head injuries
Tennessee State Tigers defender Christion Abercrombie underwent emergency surgery after suffering head injuries in Saturday's 31-27 loss to the Vanderbilt Commodores, Tigers' Mike Organ said.
Tennessee team coach Rod Reed told reporters that the injury occurred just before halftime.
"He approached the sideline and practically fainted," Reed reported.
The technical team and medical personnel put Abercrombie oxygen on the sideline before laying it on a stretcher and transporting it for further evaluation.
A team representative told Chris Harris of WSMV in Nashville, Tennessee that Abercrombie was recovering from surgery at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris added that "there's still no information on the type/gravity of the injury" and that the team is trying to figure out when it occurred.
Abercrombie, a second-year student, suspended studies during the first season with the Tennessee State Tigers following an Illinois transfer.
He made five scores on Saturday before leaving the game, which raises his total of the season to 18 scores.
A higher stamp duty will be levied on foreign buyers when purchasing properties in the UK
The Conservative Party has announced plans to levy a higher stamp duty on foreign buyers who acquire property in the UK, with the additional revenue to be vested in aid to the homeless
The move will neutralize the success of Corbyn's campaign to lure the younger electorate
Rising seal tax will focus on buyers who are not taxed in the UK
The finance ministry expects to raise up to 120 million pounds a year to help the homeless
It will be levied a higher seal tax rate on foreign buyers who acquire properties in the UK, with the additional revenue to be invested in aid to the homeless, Theresa May will announce today.
The move is seen as an attempt to counteract Jeremy Corbyn’s campaign’s success to lure the younger electorate, with pledges to provide more affordable accommodation and tax higher incomes.
Rising the stamp duty will focus on people and businesses that are not taxed in the UK, with additional revenue boosting the government's campaign to offer a ceiling to homeless.
The surcharge - added to the current stamp duty, including the highest levels introduced two years ago for the second dwellings and lease purchases - could amount to the three percent.
The Ministry of Finance expects the measure to allow it to raise up to 120 million pounds a year.
It is estimated that 13% of new builds in London will be acquired by non-residents in the UK, driving up prices and making it difficult for first-home buyers to access to the market.
Many thrish zones of the country - especially in the capital - have become "ghost cities" due to the high number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the country.
The new policy comes weeks after Boris Johnson has demanded a seal tax cut to help young people acquire their first housing.
It has accused the large construction companies of keeping property prices elevated by hoarding land they don't use, requiring Ms. May to abandon quotas on affordable housing to resolve the "disgrace of accommodation" in Britain.
Mr Corbyn has announced an appellating set of housing reform proposals, including rent controls and an end to evictions "without justification."
It also wants to allocate extended powers to municipalities for the construction of new houses.
Ms May said: "Last year I stated that I would dedicate my mandate to restoring the British dream that living conditions should be improved for every new generation.
And that means solving the problem of our housing market.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and build their life here.
However, it is not certain that it will be as easy to buy home for people living outside the UK and for foreign companies as for resident British workers.
For too many people the dream of owning their home has become too far away and the shame of those who sleep in the open remains too real.”
Jack Ross: "My ultimate ambition is to guide Scotland"
Sunderland coach Jack Ross has stated that his "ultimate ambition" is to become Scotland's coach.
The 42-year-old Scot has been successful in the challenge of giving new life to the north-eastern club, the reigning third-ranked Premier League, three-point lead.
He transferred to the Stadium of Light this summer after mentoring St. Mirren on his return to the Scottish First Division last season.
“I wanted to represent my country as a player.
But I've only achieved a B internationalisation," Ross told BBC Scotland's Sportsound programme.
"I've grown up watching Scotland play in Hampden with my father and those are some of my best memories.
But that opportunity will only arise if it succeeds in managing clubs.”
Ross' predecessors as Sunderland coach include Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa Athletic coach says he was not afraid to succeed such well-established names at such a large club after rejecting invitations from Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
"For me, at the moment the success boils down to "can I take the club back to the Premier League?"
Considering the structure and facilities of the club, their place is undoubtedly in the Premier League," he said.
"It won't be easy to take the club back, but I'll only consider that I've been successful here when I reach that goal."
Ross' career as a coach is just three years old, after a period as Dumbarton's assistant coach and 15 months in Hearts' coaching staff.
It then helped Alloa to recover from descent to the third division and led St. Mirren from the threat of demotion to the title of champion the following season.
Ross says he feels more comfortable now than he's ever felt during his playing career at Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St. Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
Taking care of Alloa "was a real crossroads," he recalls.
“I’ve always seen myself more in the role of coach than I did as a player.
It sounds strange because it went smoothly, I made a living as a player and had some high moments in my career.
But playing can be very tough.
We face a lot of challenges every week.
I still face them in terms of the stress and pressures of the position, but I feel very good as a coach.
I’ve always wanted to train and now that I’m doing it, I feel more comfortable than I’ve ever felt throughout my adult life.”
You can listen to the full interview at Sportsound on Radio Scotland on Sunday 30 September between 12:00 and 13:00 BST
The perfect time for a pint is 17:30 on Saturday
The summer heatwave filled British pubs, but put more pressure on restaurant chains.
Catering groups saw 2.7 percent growth in sales in July, but restaurant occupancy decreased by 4.8 percent, statistics show.
Peter Martin, of the CGA consultant, who compiled the statistics, said: "England's continuation of good weather and England's career at the longer-than-expected World Cup have caused the standard of the month of June, in which pubs saw a 2.8 percent growth in sales if it was repeated in July, but restaurants knew a pullback.
The 1.8 percent drop in catering sales in June worsened in July.
Pubs and bars with the business based on beverages had by far the best performance, with a year-on-year growth that more than compensated for the descent of restaurants.
Pubs with business based on meals have also experienced difficulties, although not as dramatic as those of catering operators.
Looks like people just wanted to go out for a drink.
In pubs and bars managed directly, beverage sales rose by 6.6 percent over the course of the month, with meals retreating 3 percent."
Paul Newman, an analyst in the leisure and catering sectors RSM, said: “These results prolong the trend we’ve seen since the end of April.
The meteorology and the impact of major social and sporting events remain the main factors that influence the sales of the out-of-home food market.
It is no surprise that the restoration groups continue to face difficulties, although the annual sales drop of 4.8% is especially harmful when added to the extension of cost pressure.
Long and hot summer couldn't arrive at the worst time for operators with the business based on meals. Only the weather will tell if the milder temperatures we know in the month of August will provide the much-desired relief."
Growth in total sales of pubs and restaurants, including new openings, was 2.7 per cent in July, reflecting the slowdown in the launch of brands.
Coffer Peach Tracker, the sector indicator of sales of British pubs, bars and restaurants, collects and analyzes the performance data of 47 operating groups, with a combined turnover of more than 9 billion pounds, being recognised as the benchmark of the sector.
One in five children hidden from their parents secret social media accounts
One in five children - some just 11 years old - have secret accounts on social media it hides from parents and teachers, a study revealed
A survey of 20,000 high school students has revealed a growth of "fake" pages on Instagram
The news aggravates fears regarding the publication of content of a sexual nature
Twenty percent of the students claimed to have a "principal" account to show their parents
One in five children - some at just 11 years old - creates social media accounts it hides from adults.
A survey of 20,000 high school students revealed a rapid growth of "fake" accounts on photo-sharing site Instagram.
The news aggravates fears about the publication of content of a sexual nature.
Twenty percent of the students claimed to have an innocuous "principal" account to show their parents, simultaneously owning private accounts.
A mother who found her 13-year-old daughter's secret account discovered that the teenager incited others to "violate" her.
The study, conducted by Digital Awareness UK and the Conference of Directors (HMC) of independent schools, revealed that 40% of young people between the ages of 11 and 18 had two profiles, half of which admitted to holding private accounts.
HMC director Mike Buchanan said: “It’s disturbing that so many teenagers are tempted to create online spaces where their parents and teachers can’t find them.”
