Dairy extraction pump announcel compares mothers to cows by splitting opinions online
A milk-extracting pump company has divided opinions online with an ad that compares mothers who breastfeed to cows in milking.
To mark the launch of what is described as the "first portable silent milk extraction pump," consumer technology company Elvie has released an ironic announcement inspired by the music videos to highlight the freedom conferred by the new pump on mothers who extract milk.
Four mothers dance in a hay-filled cowery to the sound of a song that includes verses like: "Yes, I'm milking myself, but I don't have a tail" and "If you haven't noticed, I don't have tits, these are my breasts."
The chorus continues: "Dumb, pump, feed the babies, pump, pump, I'm milking my girls."
However, the ad posted on the company's Facebook page has sparked online controversy.
With 77,000 views and hundreds of comments, the video has received mixed reactions from viewers, with many saying it lightens the "horrors" of the dairy sector.
"Sare decision to use cows to announce this product.
Like us, they have to get pregnant and give birth to produce milk, but their babies are stolen from them a few days after they are born.”
The Elvie extraction pump fits discreetly inside a breastfeeding bra (Elvie/Mother)
Another commented: "Understandably traumatic for the mother and the baby.
But yes, why not use them to announce an extraction pump for mothers who can stay with their babies?"
Someone further said, "An ad with a huge lack of sensibility."
Others defended the ad, like a woman who admitted to finding the song "hilarious."
"I think the idea is great.
If you were still breast-feeding, I'd buy one.
The extraction made me feel exactly like a cow.
The ad's kind of crazy, but it makes perfect sense.
This product is brilliant," one of the women wrote.
Another commented: "It's an amusing ad aimed at mothers who extract milk (often in bathrooms or in the workplace) and feel like "cows."
It's not an ad praising or judging the dairy sector."
At the end of the video the group of women reveals that they have been dancing with the discreet bombs lodged in the bras.
The concept behind the campaign builds on the information that many women who extract milk with pumps claim to feel like cows.
However, the Elvie pump is completely silent, has no wires or tubes and fits discreetly inside a breastfeeding bra, giving women the freedom to move around, pick up their babies and even leave the house while extracting milk.
Ana Balarin, partner and Creative Director of "Mother", commented: "The Elvie pump is such a revolutionary product that it deserved a daring and provocative release.
By establishing a parallel between women who extract milk and dairy cows we wanted to highlight all the challenges of milk extraction, while presenting in a fun way and with which people can identify the incredible sense of freedom provided by the new pump.
It wasn't the first time the Elvie bomb had made headlines.
During London Fashion Week, a mother of two paraded at the catwalk for designer Marta Jakubowski while using the product.
