The risk increased in June when federal authorities announced that potential godparents and other adults entering their households would have to record their fingerprints, the data being shared with immigration-regulating authorities.
Last week Matthew Albence, a senior employee of the Immigration and Customs Control Service, testified before Congress that the service held dozens of people who applied to sponsor unaccompanied minors.
The service later confirmed that 70% of the detainees had no criminal record.
"Close to 80% of godparents or members of their respective households find themselves illegal in the country, many of them foreigners with a criminal record.
So we're going to continue chasing these individuals," said Mr. Albence.
Seeking to expedite the processing of children, officials introduced new rules requiring some of them to present themselves in court within one month from the date of detention, instead of 60 days, the previous rule, according to reception centre employees.
Many are present by videoconference, and not in person, to defend their application for legal status before a judge of the immigration court.
Those considered ineligible for aid will be promptly deported.
The longer the children remain detained, the more likely they are to feel anxious or depressed, which can lead to access to violence or escape attempts, according to the employees of the reception centers and with reports produced by the system in recent months.
Immigrant rights advocates say these concerns are exacerbated in larger facilities such as Tornillo's, where the sheer size contributes to children's signs of disruption being ignored.
They add that the transfer of the children to the camps without leaving time for their emotional preparation or to say goodbye to friends aggravates the trauma that many are already struggling with.
