Representatives of the State Department decided to call the leaders of the U.S. diplomatic representations in El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and Panama following “recent decisions to stop recognizing Taiwan,” Heather Nauert, a State Department spokeswoman, said in a statement this month.
But it was only considered sanctions against El Salvador, which received about 140 million dollars of U.S. aid in 2017, including for the control of drug trafficking, economic aid and development.
