His reports were corroborated by an external analyst close to the Administration, who also requested anonymity.
Study history
The next bomb to be dropped could be legal adviser Robert Mueller’s report on Mr. Trump’s possible obstruction of justice, about which there is currently substantial evidence in the public domain.
Mueller is reportedly directing his investigation to ascertain whether Trump's campaign conspired with Russia to attack the elections.
In case Congress changes hands, Trump will face accountability on the part of that body, as he prepares to present himself again to voters and perhaps to be tried by his peers.
It's a lot of hypotheses and I don't want to suggest that Mr. Trump's downfall is inevitable - nor that of his counterparts in Europe.
There are choices we have to make on both sides of the Atlantic that will affect the duration of combat.
In 1983, some German officers were prepared to depose Hitler in a coup, as long as the West resisted and supported the Czechoslovaks in Munich.
We failed and missed the opportunity to avoid the years of carnage that followed.
The course of history revolves around tipping points like this, in which the inexorable march of democracy is accelerated or fought.
Americans currently face several of these tipping points.
What we'll do if Trump fires Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, the man who controls the fate of Mr. Mueller's investigation?
Rosenstein has been under threat ever since this newspaper reported that last year he suggested secretly recording the President, speculating about his inability to hold the post.
Rosenstein says The Times news is incorrect.
"How will we respond if the FBI's recently requested investigation into Brett Kavanaugh is not fair and complete or if it is confirmed to the Supreme Court despite credible accusations of sexual abuse and dishonest testimony?
And most of all, will we vote in the midterm elections for a Congress that holds Mr. Trump accountable?
If we fail in these tests, democracy will be about to traverse a long desert.
But I believe we won't fail, because of the lesson I learned in Prague.
My mother was a Checoslovak Jewish woman who was deported to Auschwitz by the same Nazi regime who once occupied my diplomatic residence.
She survived, immigrated to America and, 60 years later, ordered me to light Sabbath candles at that table with the swastika.
With such an inheritance, how could it not be optimistic about our future?”
Norman Eisen, a senior researcher at the Brookings Institution, is president of Citizens for Responsity and Ethics in Washington and author of "The Last Palace: Europe's Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House."
