Women are losing sleep for fear of this savings-reform account
Despite the fact that the participants in the survey had a clear idea about the type of care they want to enjoy, very few people spoke to their family members about it.
About half of the individuals questioned in the Nationwide study reported talking to their spouses about the cost of continuing care.
Only 10% mentioned talking to their children about the same subject.
“People want a family member to take care of themselves, but they’re not having that conversation,” said Holly Snyder, vice president of Nationwide’s life insurance business.
Here's how to get started.
Speak with your spouse and your children: You will not be able to prepare your family to provide care if you do not let them know their wishes in advance.
Work with your consultant and your family, discussing where and how to receive the care, since these choices can constitute a significant factor in determining costs.
Involve your financial advisor: Your advisor can also help you find a way to pay for those expenses.
Your continuing care financing choices may include a long-term traditional insurance policy, a currency-raised hybrid policy to help cover those expenses, or funding using your own savings as long as you have those amounts available.
Prepare legal documentation: Avoid legal battles.
Appoint a health care attorney by choosing a trusted person to supervise their health care and make sure professionals respect their wishes if they’re prevented from communicating them.
Also consider the constitution of a power of attorney about your finances.
You should select someone you trust to make financial decisions for yourself by ensuring that your bills will be paid if you become incapacitated.
Don’t forget the small details: Imagine that your elderly parent suffers a medical emergency and is on their way to the hospital.
Do you know how to answer questions about medication and allergies?
Include this information in a written plan to be prepared.
