earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people were killed by a strong earthquake and tsunami that hit the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, officials said, and the death toll was expected to rise.
With communications interrupted, first responders were unable to obtain any information from the Donggala region, a zone north of Palu that is closest to the epicenter of the 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
In Palu, more than 16,000 people were evacuated after the disaster occurred.
Here are some key facts about Palu and Donggala on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulawesi province, located at the end of a narrow bay on the western coast of Sulawesi island, with an estimated population of 379,800 inhabitants in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when the earthquake and tsunami took place.
Donggala is a region that extends along more than 300 km of coastline, in the north-western island of Sulawesi.
The region, with administrative status lower than that of a province, had an estimated population of 299,200 inhabitants in 2017.
Fishing and agriculture are the main economic activities of Central Sulawesi Province, especially in the Donggala coastal region.
Nickel extraction is also important in the province, but it concentrates mostly in Morowali on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been hit by tsunamis several times over the past hundred years, according to the Indonesian disaster management agency.
In 1938, a tsunami killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of homes in Donggala.
A tsunami also hit the western part of Donggala in 1996, killing nine people.
Indonesia is located in the Pacific Seismic Ring of Fire, being regularly hit by earthquakes.
Here are some of the main earthquakes and tsunamis of recent years:
2004: On December 26 a strong earthquake on the western coast of the Indonesian province of Aceh in northern Sumatra originated a Tsunamis that hit 14 countries, killing 226,000 people along the Indian Ocean coast, more than half of them in Aceh.
2005: A series of strong earthquakes reached the western coast of Sumatra in late March and early April.
Hundreds of people have died on Nias Island off Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude earthquake hit southern Java, Indonesia's most populous island, originating a Tsunamis that fell on the southern coast, killing nearly 700 people.
2009: A 7.6-magnitude earthquake hit the city of Padang, capital of Western Sumatra province.
More than 1,100 people have died.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude earthquake hit one of the Mentawai islands off Sumatra, triggering a tsunami with waves of up to 10 meters that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A shallow earthquake hit Aceh’s Pidie Jaya region, spreading destruction and panic among people who remembered the devastation caused by 2004’s deadly earthquake and tsunami.
This time there was no tsunami, but more than 100 people died buried under the rubble of the buildings.
2018: Large earthquakes hit the tourist Indonesian island of Lombok, killing more than 500 people, mostly on the north side of the island.
Earthquakes have destroyed thousands of buildings, leaving thousands of tourists temporarily isolated.
Sarah Palin's eldest son arrested on domestic violence charges
Track Palin, the eldest son of former Alaska governor and vice-presidency candidate Sarah Palin, has been detained on assault charges.
Palin, a 29-year-old resident in Wasilla, Alaska, has been detained on suspicion of domestic violence, interference in an investigation into domestic violence and resistance to detention, according to a report released Saturday by Alaskan police.
