Public prosecutor's office calls for rare capital punishment for New York terror attack suspect
The New York prosecutor's office calls for the death penalty for Sayfullo Saipov, the suspect in the terrorist attack that killed eight people in the city - a rare sentence that hasn't been executed in the state for a federal crime since 1953.
Saipov, 30, is alleged to have used a truck leased by Home Depot to carry out an attack in October on a bike path on the West Side Highward in Lower Manhattan, trampling pedestrians and cyclists.
To justify the death penalty, prosecutors will have to prove that Saipov "intentionally" killed the eight victims and intentionally inflicted serious injuries, according to the notification requesting the death penalty, registered in the South Zone of New York.
Both charges can be punished with the death penalty, according to the court document.
Weeks after the attack, a federal jury accused Saipov of 22 crimes, including eight counts of murder in the service of organized crime, normally used by the prosecutor's office in cases of organized crime, and an accusation of violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack required "considerable planning and premeditation," prosecutors said, describing the way Saipov executed him as "atrocious, cruel and depraved."
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injuries, damage and loss to the family and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco," the notification said.
Five of the victims were Argentinian tourists.
It's been a decay since New York's South Zone asked for the death penalty for an indictment.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was convicted of murdering two drug traffickers, but the sentence was commuted to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The last time the death penalty was executed in a federal case in New York was in 1953, when the couple Julius and Ethel Rosenberg were executed after being convicted of conspiracy to commit espionage acts in favor of the Soviet Union, two years earlier, during the Cold War.
The Rosenbergs were executed in the electric chair on June 19, 1953.
According to the court’s documentation, Uzbekistan native Saipov did not demonstrate any remorses in the days and months that followed the attack.
Declared investigators to feel good about what they had done, police said.
Saipov told authorities he had been inspired to carry out the attack after seeing ISIS videos on his phone, according to the indictment.
He also requested authorization to hang the ISIS flag in the hospital room, police said.
He pleaded not guilty in relation to all 22 charges.
David Patton, one of Saipov's defense lawyers, said they were "obviously disappointed" by the prosecution's decision.
“We think that the decision to seek the death penalty instead of accepting a confession of guilt for life imprisonment, without possibility of release, will only prolong the trauma associated with these events for everyone involved,” Patton said.
Saipov's defense team had previously asked accusers not to seek the death penalty.
Conservative MP claims NIGEL FARAGE should lead Brexit talks
Nigel Farage today vowed to "mobilize the people's army" during a protest at the Conservatives congress.
The former UKIP leader said politicians should "feel the pressure" of Euroskeptics, while one of Theresa May's MPs suggested that he should lead negotiations with the EU.
Conservative rear MP Peter Bone claimed at the Birmingham rally that Britain "would have already left" if Farage was Brexit minister.
But the challenge faced by Ms. May to reconcile her deeply divided co-religionists was underlined by the Conservatives advocating the permanence, who organised a separate protest against Brexit in the city.
The prime minister has struggled to maintain her compromise plan, known as the Chequers Plan, in the face of attacks by supporters of Brexit, advocates of permanence and the EU.
Allies insist that she will continue to try to reach an agreement with Brussels despite protests, forcing Eurosceptics and the Labour Party to choose between their package and "chaos."
Mr. Bone said at the Leave Means Leave rally in Solihull that he intended to "end the Plan."
Bone suggested that Farage's responsibilities should have been assigned in negotiations with Brussels.
“If he was responsible, we would have already left,” he said.
Wellingborough MP added: "I will continue to defend Brexit but we should end the Plan."
Strengthening his opposition to the EU declared: "We have not fought in two World Wars to be subservient.
We want to make the laws of our own country.”
Bone declined allegations that public opinion has changed since the 2016 referendum: "The idea that the British people have changed their minds and intend to remain is utterly misplaced."
Conservative Brexit supporter Andrea Jenkyns also took part in the demonstration, stating to journalists: "I simply say: Madam Prime Minister, listen to the people.
The Chequers Plan is unpopular to the public, the opposition will not approve it, it is unpopular in our own party and with the militants who take the campaigns to the street and to whom we owe our election.
Drop the Chequers Plan and start listening.”
In a message addressed to Ms. May, he added: "Only the prime ministers who know how to keep the promises keep their jobs."
Farage said at the rally that politicians should "feel the pressure" if they print themselves to betray the decision exit from the 2016 referendum.
“This has become a matter of trust between us – the people – and the political class,” he said.
"They're trying to betray Brexit and we're here today to tell them they're not getting away with it."
Addressing the enthusiastic crowd, he added: "I want them to put pressure on our political class, which is on the verge of betraying Brexit.
We want to mobilise the people's army of this country that has given us victory in Brexit and that will not rest until we become an independent, proud United Kingdom that governs itself."
However, advocates of permanence marched in Birmingham before organizing a two-hour rally in the city centre.
A crowd of activists wielded banners with the message "Conservatives against Brexit" following the group's launch this weekend.
Labour's representative in the House of Lords Andrew Adonis criticised the Conservatives for the security issues they faced with a party application at the opening of the congress.
"It's these people who tell us they're going to have computer systems and all the technology prepared for 'Canada more', for the frictionless border, for free trade without borders in Ireland," he added.
It's a complete hoax.
There's no good Brexit," he concluded.
