Arrest on the murder of Wayde Sims of LSU Tigers
Police in Baton Rouge, Louisiana, announced Saturday the detention of a suspect in the murder of LSU Tigers basketball player Wayde Sims on Friday.
Baton Rouge Police announced the arrest of 20-year-old Dyteon Simpson at a press conference at 11 a.m.
Police had released a video of the shooting on Friday, asking for help in identifying a man seen in the footage.
Sims, 20, was shot down near the Southern University campus early on Friday.
"Wayde Sims was shot in the head, in the wake of which he eventually died," police chief Murphy J. Paul was quoted as saying by 247sports on Saturday.
Wayde defended a friend and was shot by Simpson.
Simpson was questioned and admitted his presence at the scene, wielding a weapon, acknowledging to have targeted Wayde Sims.
Simpson was detained without presenting resistance and taken to East Baton Rouge Parish police precinct.
Sims, a 1.98-meter junior raised in Baton Rouge, played 32 games and started 10 times last season, with an average of 17.4 minutes, 5.6 points and 2.9 recoveries per game.
Rusian Grand Prix: Lewis Hamilton approaches world title with a win over Sebastian Vettel following team orders
It became clear from the moment Valtteri Bottas qualified in front of Lewis Hamilton on Saturday that team orders at Mercedes would play a central role in the race.
Leaving pole position, Bottas had a good starter and left Hamilton behind when defending his position in the first two corners, allowing Vettel to attack his teammate.
Vettel went first to the pits and caused Hamilton to have to face traffic from the end of the peloton, which could have been decisive.
The Mercedes went to the pits one lap later and left behind Vettel, but Hamilton surpassed him after a tight skirmish, after which the Ferrari driver reluctantly left the internal part of the free track, at risk of disqualification after a double trajectory correction to defend himself in the third corner.
Max Verstappen came out of the last line of the grid and was in the seventh place at the end of the first lap, on the day of his 21st birthday.
He then led much of the race, switching too late from tyres, which would allow him to be fast in the final, aiming to overtake Kimi Raikkonen to finish fourth.
He eventually came to the pits on the 44th lap but couldn't improve his pace in the last eight laps, which allowed Raikkonen to finish fourth.
"It's a difficult day because Valtteri did a fantastic job throughout the weekend and was a true gentleman, letting me get past him.
The team did an exceptional job, which allowed us to end up in the first two positions," Hamilton said.
Poor body language
