We see the wickedness, the malice, it doesn't matter who they can reach, whoever they have to step on to take power and control," the president said, according to Mediaite.
First Division: Dundee Stars defeat for Belfast Giants 5-3
Patrick Dwyer scored two goals for the Giants against Dundee
The Dundee Stars redeemed themselves from the defeat on Friday in the First Division game against the Belfast Giants, winning the second leg 5-3 on Saturday at Dundee.
The Giants got an initial two-goal lead with shots from Patrick Dwyer and Francis Beauvillier.
Mike Sullivan and Jordan Cownie tied for the home team before Dwyer re-putted the Giants in front.
François Bouchard drew for Dundee before two goals from Lukas Lundvald secured the team's victory.
It was the season's third loss to the men of Adam Keefe, who had managed a turnaround on Friday night in Belfast, defeating Dundee 2-1.
It was the fourth meeting of the season between the two teams, and the Giants won the previous three games.
Dwyer's first goal came in the fourth minute (3:35) in the wake of an assist by Kendall McFaull, with David Rutherford watching for Beauvillier raise the lead four minutes later.
In a frenzied first period, Sullivan returned to put the home team into the game at 13:10 before Matt Marquardt gave Cownie the draw at 15:16.
Dwyer made sure the Giants got to the first break in the lead, scoring their second goal of the night at the end of the first period.
The home team reorganized and Bouchard drew again with a mighty goal at 27:37.
Cownie and Charles Corcoran combined efforts to help Nielsen lead Dundee to the lead for the first time in the game at the end of the second period, securing victory by scoring the team's fifth goal in the middle of the last period.
The Giants, who have lost for four of their last five games, welcome Milton Keynes in the next game on Friday.
Aerial controller dies to help hundreds of passengers evade earthquake
An Indonesian air controller has been hailed as a hero after he died while ensuring that a plane with hundreds of passengers took off safely.
More than 800 people have died and many remain missing after a strong earthquake struck the island of Sulawesi on Friday, stemming a tsunami.
Potent replicas continue to ravage the region, leaving many residents buried in the rubble in the town of Palu.
But even though his colleagues fled to save themselves, 21-year-old Anthonius Gunawan Agung refused to leave his post while the control tower wagged violently at Palu's Mutiara Sis Al Jufri airport.
It remained focused to ensure that Batik Air flight 6321, which was then on the runway, took off safely.
He then jumped out of the control tower in the face of the eminent collapse.
He later died in the hospital.
Indonesian air navigation agency spokesman Yohannes Sirait said the decision could have saved hundreds of lives, Australia's ABC News reported.
We sent a Helicopter from Balikpapan in Kalimantan to evacuate him to a larger hospital in another city.
Unfortunately, he passed away this morning before the helicopter arrived in Palu.
"We were devastated by this news," he added.
However, the authorities fear that the number of deaths could reach the thousands, referring to the country’s disaster management agency that access to the cities of Donggala, Sigi and Boutong is limited.
"The swing is increasing, since there are many bodies buried by the rubble that we have not yet been able to rescue," the spokesman for Sutopo Purwo Nugroho reported.
Waves of up to six meters devastated Palu, where a collective funeral is held on Sunday.
Commercial and military planes are transporting aid and groceries to the site.
Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother, told Sky News: "Every minute ambulances with bodies arrive.
Lacked drinking water.
Mini-markets are being plundered everywhere.”
Jan Gelfand, head of the International Red Cross in Indonesia, told CNN: "The Red Cross Indonesia is running against time to help the survivors, but we don't know what we're going to find.
The current situation is really tragic, but it could be much worse.”
Indonesian President Joko Widodo arrived in Palu on Sunday. According to CNN sources, he addressed the military forces at the scene and said:
"I ask you to work day and night to complete the evacuation operations. Are you prepared?"
Earlier this year, Lombok Island in Indonesia was hit by earthquakes that took the lives of more than 550 people.
