New-Yorthich nanny sues couple for dismissal via "racist" SMS message
A New York nanny is suing a couple for discriminatory dismissal after she mistakenly received an SMS message from her mother complaining that she "was more of a black one."
The couple denied accusations of racism, stating that the process was an act of "extortion."
Lynsey Plasco-Flaxman, a mother of two, showed some fear when she discovered that the new nanny Giselle Maurice was black when she introduced herself for her first day of work in 2016.
"I CAN'T BELIEVE... ANOTHER BLACK ONE," Lynsey Plasco-Flaxman wrote to her husband in an SMS message.
However, instead of sending the message to her husband, he sent it to Giselle Maurice... twice.
According to the New York Post, when she realized her gaffe, an "embarrassed" Lynsey Plasco-Flaxman fired Giselle Maurice claiming the poor performance of the previous African-American nanny and that she was waiting for a Filipino nanny.
Giselle Maurice was paid for her only working day and sent home from Uber.
Now Giselle Maurice is suing the couple and claiming compensation for a redundancy of 350 USD/day in relation to the six-month period for which he had been chosen without a contract.
“I want to show them that they can’t go unpunished,” he told the Post on Friday, adding, “I know this is discrimination.”
The couple refuted allegations of racism, saying firing Giselle was perfectly logical since they were afraid they couldn't trust her after they offended her.
"My wife sent you something I really didn't want to say.
It's not racist.
We're not racist people," Joel Plasco told the Post.
"Would your children entrust to someone they'd treated badly?
Your newborn son?
I don't believe."
In referencing the "extortion" process, Plasco said his wife had been a mother just two months ago and had to deal with "a very difficult situation."
"Will they chase someone like that?
It's not very sympathetic," the investment banker added.
While the legal process takes place, the court of public opinion was swift in convicting the couple on social media, criticizing their behavior and reasoning.
