Number of North Korean defectors declines during Kim regime
According to a South Korean legislator, the number of North Korean defectors for North Korea has declined since Kim Jong-un's rise to power seven years ago.
Citing data from South Korea’s Unification Ministry, Park Byeong-seug indicated 1127 defections last year compared to 2706 in 2011.
Park stated that stricter border controls between North Korea and China, as well as the fact that smugglers of people asking for more money, were the main factors.
Pyongyang has not made any public statements.
As a general rule, South Korean citizenship is granted to the vast majority of North Korea's defectors.
Seoul disclosed that more than 30,000 North Koreans illegally passed the border since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
The majority flee through China, which has the longest border with North Korea and is easier to traverse than the heavily protected Demilitarized Zone (DMZ) between the two Koreas.
China considers defectors to be illegal emigrants and not refugees, and it is often to repatriate them.
Relations between North and South Korea (technically still at war) have improved significantly in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries met in Pyongyang to debate the denuclearization talks that are at an impasse.
The talks took place after the historic meeting in June between U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, during which they agreed, in general, to collaborate in the creation of a denuclearized Korean peninsula.
However, on Saturday, North Korean Foreign Ministers Ri Yong-ho blamed the U.S. for a lack of progress.
“Without trust in the U.S., there will be no confidence in our national security, and in these circumstances it is impossible for us to take the first steps towards unilateral disarmament,” Ri said in a speech at the United Nations Assembly in New York.
