Foreign Minister Says Syria Is Prepared for Refugee Return
Syria claims to be prepared for the voluntary return of refugees and calls for aid for the reconstruction of a country devastated by more than seven long years of war.
In his speech at the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem stated that the country’s conditions are improving.
"Nowadays, the situation on the ground is more stable and secure thanks to progress in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government continues to rehabilitate areas destroyed by terrorists to replenish normalcy.
There are now all the conditions for the voluntary return of refugees to the country they have abandoned due to terrorism and the unilateral economic measures that have affected their daily lives and livelihoods.
The United Nations estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled the country since the start of the war in 2011.
Another six million people who have remained in the country need humanitarian aid.
Al-Moualem said the Syrian regime would gladly receive aid for the reconstruction of the devastated country.
However, he stressed that he would not accept conditional assistance or help from the countries that sponsored the revolt.
Europe wins Ryder Cup in Paris
The European national team beats the U.S. and wins the 2018 Ryder Cup with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 at Le Golf National on the outskirts of Paris, France.
The U.S. has now racked up six consecutive defeats on European soil since they last won the Ryder Cup in Europe in 1993.
Europe again crowned itself victorious after Danish captain Thomas Bjorn's team reached the 14.5 points they needed to beat the United States.
American star Phil Mickelson, who has been shown to have serious difficulties during almost the entire tournament, hurled the starting tee into the water in hole 16, par 3, giving Francesco Molinari victory.
Italian golfer Molinari shone in all his rounds, becoming 1 of the 4 players getting 5-0-0 since the tournament's current format began in 1979.
American Jordan Spieth lost 5&4 to European national team player Thorbjorn Olesen of Denmark.
The world's highest-rated player, Dustin Johnson, has lost 2 and 1 to England's Ian Poulter in what may have been his last Ryder Cup appearance.
An eight-Ryder Cups veteran, Spaniard Sergio Garcia became the most awarded European of all tournaments with 25.5 career points.
“It’s not my habit to cry, but today I couldn’t contain myself.
It's been a tough year.
I can't help but thank Thomas for choosing and believing in me.
I'm immensely happy that we won the cup.
It's all been a team effort and I'm happy to have been able to help," an emotive Garcia claimed after Europe's victory.
He passes the word to his compatriot Jon Rham, who defeated U.S. Golf legend Tiger Woods for 2&1 in singulars on Sunday.
"It's impossible to describe the pride I feel for having beaten Tiger Woods. I've grown up watching him play," said 23-year-old Rahm.
Woods have lost all four games in France and now have a 13-21-3 record in the Ryder Cup.
Unusual statistical values of one of the greatest players of all time with 14 big titles won and only beaten by Jack Nicklaus.
With the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau who had a high-caliber performance throughout the tournament, the remaining elements of the U.S
U.S. captain Jim Furyk spoke after his team's disappointing performance. "I'm proud of them. They fought.
This tomorrow, we'll be able to put some fear into Europe.
Demos fights.
My congratulations to Thomas.
You're a great captain.
All of his 12 players were excellent.
We're going to reorganize and I'll collaborate with the PGA of America and our Ryder Cup Commission to move on.
I love these 12 players and I'm proud to be their captain.
I have to take my hat off the other team.
They were better."
Updated Red Tide information: Concentrations decrease in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
According to new information from the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission, there was a decrease in Red Tide concentrations in some areas of Tampa Bay.
FWC has communicated that conditions of lower spot proliferation have been identified in areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier counties, suggesting a decrease in concentrations.
The patch of the Red Tide extends about 201 km along the coast, from Pinellas county to the north to Lee County to the south.
Spots can be found about 16 km off the coast of Hillsborough County, but in fewer areas than last week.
Red Tide was also sighted in Pasco County.
Last week, average concentrations were recorded near or off the coast of Pinella County, low to high concentrations off the coast of Hillsborough County, bottom-to-high concentrations in Manatee County, bottom-to-high concentrations near or off the coast of Sarasota County, bottom-to-medium concentrations in Charlotte County, bottom-to-high concentrations near or off the coast of Lee County, and low concentrations in Collier County.
There are still reports of respiratory irritations in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier counties.
In the last week, there have been no reports of respiratory irritations in the northwest Florida region.
